eq 0 for all  x . Then  Y has density f_{Y}(y)=f_{X} igl{(}h(y) igr{)}\, igl{|}h^{\prime}(y) igr{|} for all  y in the range of  g . Proof. We only consider the case where g is increasing, the proof for the decreasing case is similar with changes in sign. The distribution function of  Y is given by F_{Y}(y)=\mathbb{P}(g(X)\leq y)=\mathbb{P} igl{(}X\leq h(y) igr{)}=F_{X}%  igl{(}h(y) igr{)}, where we used that g is increasing and has inverse  h . Taking derivatives with respect to  y , using the chain rule, we find f_{Y}(y)=f_{X}(h(y))\,h^{\prime}(y) and thus h^{\prime}(y)>0 equals its own absolute value as given in the formula. This completes the proof. ∎ The same argument applies to a vector of random variables: if X has a joint density f_{X} on  \mathbb{R}^{k} and Y=g(X) for a differentiable, one-to-one map  g with inverse  h , then Y has joint density f_{Y}(y)=f_{X} igl{(}h(y) igr{)}\,|\det h^{\prime}(y)| , where h^{\prime}(y) is the Jacobian matrix of  h . For a linear map with determinant one, as in the solution to exercise 10.2 in appendix C, the factor is  1 . A.8 Bayes’ Theorem The final tool reverses the direction of conditioning, expressing the conditional density of one variable given another in terms of the reverse conditional. It is the mechanism behind every posterior distribution in the Bayesian lectures. Proposition A.20 (Bayes’ theorem). Let A and  B be random variables with joint density f(a,b) and let B have strictly positive marginal density  f(b) . Then the conditional density of  A given B=b is f(a\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mub)= rac{f(b\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mua)\,f(a)}{f(b% )}, where f(b)=\int f(b\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mua)\,f(a)\,\mathrm{d}a is the marginal density of  B . If one of the two variables is discrete, the same formula holds with its probability weights in place of the density, and the integral in  f(b) becomes a sum over the values of  A when A is discrete. In the Bayesian lectures of the module, the unknown parameter plays the role of A with prior density f(a) and the data are given by  B . In this situation, proposition A.20 states that the posterior density is proportional to the product of the likelihood and the prior density. The denominator  f(B) does not depend on the parameter. This viewpoint is further developed in lectures 26 and 28. Previous page Next page

