m exercise 28.1. ∎ Solution to exercise 28.3. 1.​Since the prior is flat, we have 	ext{Beta}(1,1) and using n=3 and T=2 from example 26.5 we find the posterior 	ext{Beta}(3,2) . Since B(3,2)=\Gamma(3)\Gamma(2)/\Gamma(5)=2/24=1/12 , the density of this posterior is \pi(	heta\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux)=12	heta^{2}(1-	heta) for 0<	heta<1 . 2.​We can find the posterior probability of  H_{0} by integrating the density: \mathbb{P}(	heta\leq 1/2\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux)=12\int_{0}^{1/2}(	heta^{% 2}-	heta^{3})\,\mathrm{d}	heta=12\Bigl{(} rac{1}{24}- rac{1}{64}\Bigr{)}=%  rac{5}{16}=0.3125. Thus we have \mathbb{P}(H_{1}\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux)=11/16 and the posterior odds for H_{0} against H_{1} are 5/11\approx 0.45 . 3.​The data have multiplied the odds by 5/11 . Using Bayes’ theorem for the events H_{0} and  H_{1} , we find that the posterior probability of H_{j} is \mathbb{P}(H_{j}) times the average of the likelihoods over  H_{j} , with respect to the prior conditioned on  H_{j} , divided by the marginal density of the data. The factor by which the odds change is the ratio of these two averaged likelihoods. In this case, the likelihood is 	heta^{2}(1-	heta) and the conditional priors are uniform on (0,1/2) and on (1/2,1) , and thus the factor is  rac{2\int_{0}^{1/2}	heta^{2}(1-	heta)\,\mathrm{d}	heta}{2\int_{1/2}^{1}% 	heta^{2}(1-	heta)\,\mathrm{d}	heta}= rac{5/192}{1/12-5/192}= rac{5}{11}. This agrees with the result from the previous part. Note that this is not the ratio of likelihoods at two values, since each hypothesis includes a range of parameter values, and these need to be weighted by the prior before the two hypotheses can be compared. Only for two simple hypotheses do the averages coincide with single likelihood values, as in case (28.3). 4.​The bounds l and  u satisfy 12\int_{0}^{l}(	heta^{2}-	heta^{3})\,\mathrm{d}	heta=0.025 and 12\int_{0}^{u}(	heta^{2}-	heta^{3})\,\mathrm{d}	heta=0.975 , i.e. we have 4l^{3}-3l^{4}=0.025\qquad	ext{and}\qquad 4u^{3}-3u^{