pha , where the bound is achieved at the boundary. 4.​The two-sided test with level  \alpha rejects large values of  V only if V>\chi^{2}_{n-1,1-\alpha/2} . Since this is a larger threshold than the \chi^{2}_{n-1,1-\alpha} of the one-sided test, the power of the two-sided test against the alternative \sigma^{2}>\sigma_{0}^{2} is smaller than the power of the one-sided test. An observed value between the two thresholds is rejected by the one-sided test but not by the two-sided test. The two-sided test has power above  \alpha against the alternative \sigma^{2}<\sigma_{0}^{2} , where the one-sided test has probability of rejection below  \alpha . For the given data, both tests reject the hypothesis at level 0.05 , but at level 0.025 the one-sided test, with threshold \chi^{2}_{14,0.975}=26.119 , still rejects, whereas the two-sided test compares 27.07 to \chi^{2}_{14,0.9875}=28.42 and does not reject. ∎ Solution to exercise 29.3. 1.​We can describe the differences D_{i}=X_{i}-Y_{i} using i.i.d.  N(\mu_{D},\sigma_{D}^{2}) , where both parameters are unknown. We test H_{0}\colon\mu_{D}=0 against H_{1}\colon\mu_{D}>0 , since we expect the reaction time to be smaller after training. The differences are 4,-1,6,3,6,5 and the sum of these differences is 23 , i.e. we have  ar{d}=3.833 . The sum of squared deviations from   ar{d} is 34.83 , and thus we have s_{D}^{2}=34.83/5=6.967 and s_{D}=2.639 . The test statistic from proposition 29.1, for \mu_{0}=0 , is t= rac{\sqrt{6}\cdot 3.833}{2.639}=3.56, where 5 is the number of degrees of freedom. Since 3.56>t_{5,0.95}=2.015 we can reject the hypothesis at level 0.05 and since 3.56>t_{5,0.99}=3.365 we can also reject the hypothesis at level 0.01 : the data show a reduction in reaction time. 2.​The 95\% t -interval from example 25.4 for  \mu_{D} is  ar{d}\pm t_{5,0.975}\,s_{D}/\sqrt{6}=3.833\pm 2.571\cdot 2.639/2.449=3.833% \pm 2.770 , which is [1.06,6.60] . 3.​The row means are  ar{x}=419/6=69.83 and  ar{y}=396/6=66.00 , the pooled 