olimits_{	heta} igl{(}\ell_{i}^{\prime}(% 	heta) igr{)} , as in the proof of lemma 11.3, and since the observations are identically distributed, each term in the sum equals \mathop{\mathrm{Var}}
olimits_{	heta} igl{(}\ell_{1}^{\prime}(	heta) igr{% )}=\mathcal{I}(	heta). This completes the proof. ∎ 11.4 Examples We now compute the information for the standard families of tables 1.1 and 1.2. By proposition 11.4 it suffices to work with a single observation X=X_{1} . Example 11.5. For the Bernoulli distribution with success probability 	heta\in(0,1) we have \ell_{1}(	heta)=X\log	heta+(1-X)\log(1-	heta) and thus \ell_{1}^{\prime}(	heta)= rac{X}{	heta}- rac{1-X}{1-	heta},\qquad\ell_{1}% ^{\prime\prime}(	heta)=- rac{X}{	heta^{2}}- rac{1-X}{(1-	heta)^{2}}. Using \mathbb{E}_{	heta}(X)=	heta we find \mathcal{I}(	heta)=-\mathbb{E}_{	heta} igl{(}\ell_{1}^{\prime\prime}(	heta% ) igr{)}= rac{	heta}{	heta^{2}}+ rac{1-	heta}{(1-	heta)^{2}}= rac{1}{% 	heta(1-	heta)}. Thus, the information of the sample is \mathcal{I}_{n}(	heta)=n/ igl{(}	heta(1-	heta) igr{)} . Exercise 11.1 checks these identities for this model directly. Example 11.6. For the Poisson distribution with mean 	heta>0 we have \ell_{1}(	heta)=X\log	heta-	heta-\log X! and thus \ell_{1}^{\prime}(	heta)= rac{X}{	heta}-1,\qquad\ell_{1}^{\prime\prime}(% 	heta)=- rac{X}{	heta^{2}},\qquad\mathcal{I}(	heta)= rac{\mathbb{E}_{% 	heta}(X)}{	heta^{2}}= rac{1}{	heta}. Example 11.7. In the case of the normal distribution with unknown mean  \mu and known variance  \sigma^{2} , the parameter is  \mu and \ell_{1}(\mu)=-	frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi\sigma^{2})-(X-\mu)^{2}/(2\sigma^{2}) . Thus we have \ell_{1}^{\prime}(\mu)= rac{X-\mu}{\sigma^{2}},\qquad\ell_{1}^{\prime\prime}(% \mu)=- rac{1}{\sigma^{2}},\qquad\mathcal{I}(\mu)= rac{1}{\sigma^{2}}. Since the second derivative does not depend on the data, we do not need to consider the expectation. Note that \mathop{\mathrm{Var}}
olimits_{\mu}( ar{X})=\sigma^{2}/n=1/\mathcal{I}_{n}(\mu) by lemma 2.2 and from lecture 13 we know that no unbiased estimator can be better. Example 11.8. For the exponential distribution with rate  	heta>0 , we have \ell_{1}(	heta)=\log	heta-	heta X and thus \ell_{1}^{\prime}(	heta)= rac{1}{	heta}-X,\qquad\ell_{1}^{\prime\prime}(% 	heta)=- rac{1}{	heta^{2}},\qquad\mathcal{I}(	heta)= rac{1}{	heta^{2}}. Again, the second derivative is non-random. In fact, section 11.6 shows that the dependence of the information on the scale of the parameter is a general property. 11.5 Exponential Families All four models from section 11.4 are exponential families. For such models, the information is the second derivative of the cumulant function  K , given by definition 7.6. Proposition 11.9. Let f(x;\eta)=h(x)\exp igl{(}\eta T(x)-K(\eta) igr{)} be an exponential family in its natural parametrisation. Then the Fisher information of a single observation about  \eta is given by \mathcal{I}(\eta)=K^{\prime\prime}(\eta)=\mathop{\mathrm{Var}}
