eq 0 , in contradiction to lemma 11.1. The variance is zero, whereas \mathbb{E}_{	heta} igl{(}\ell_{1}^{\prime}(	heta)^{2} igr{)}=1/	heta^{2} and -\mathbb{E}_{	heta} igl{(}\ell_{1}^{\prime\prime}(	heta) igr{)}=-1/	heta^% {2} : the three expressions for lemma 11.3 take three different values, one of which is negative. The proof breaks down at the interchange between differentiation and integration: for this model, the two sides of (11.1) with k=1 differ by the term f(	heta;	heta)=1/	heta from the moving upper limit, which is missed by the differentiation under the integral sign. Exercise 11.6 can be used to work out the two computations. For this model, there is no Fisher information and the two results based on it are not applicable: the sample maximum does not satisfy the lower bound from lecture 13, the mean squared error decreases like 1/n^{2} per exercise 2.2 instead of like  1/n , and the asymptotic normality from lecture 17 also fails. Summary. •​Under the conditions of section 11.1 (a support free of  	heta , twice continuously differentiable density and differentiation under the integral sign), the score at the true parameter value has mean zero. •​The Fisher information \mathcal{I}_{n}(	heta) is the variance of the score, and it equals \mathbb{E}_{	heta} igl{(}\ell^{\prime}(	heta)^{2} igr{)} and -\mathbb{E}_{	heta} igl{(}\ell^{\prime\prime}(	heta) igr{)} , the expected curvature of the log-likelihood at the truth. •​Information from independent observations can be combined, \mathcal{I}_{n}(	heta)=n\mathcal{I}(	heta) ; for an exponential family we have \eta^{\prime}(	heta)^{2}\mathop{\mathrm{Var}}
olimits_{	heta}(T) ; under a reparametrisation \psi=g(	heta) we get \mathcal{I}_{\psi}(\psi)=\mathcal{I}_{	heta}(	heta)/g^{\prime}(	heta)^{2} . •​We have learned the following standard values: 1/ igl{(}	heta(1-	heta) igr{)} for the Bernoulli distribution, 1/	heta for the Poisson distribution, 1/\sigma^{2} for the mean of the normal distribution and 1/	heta^{2} for the rate parameter in the exponential distribution. For the uniform distribution these relations do not hold, since the support of the distribution moves with the parameter. •​The information is the currency of the two results presented later: it gives a bound on the variance of unbiased estimators in lecture 13 and it determines the asymptotic variance of the MLE in lecture 17. Exercise 11.1. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. Bernoulli with success probability 	heta\in(0,1) , and let \ell_{1}^{\prime}(	heta) be the score of a single observation from example 11.5. 1.​Verify lemma 11.1 for this model by computing \mathbb{E}_{	heta} igl{(}\ell_{1}^{\prime}(	heta) igr{)} directly from the two possible values of  X_{1} . 2.​Compute \mathop{\mathrm{Var}}
