olimits_{	heta}(W)}, where the efficiency is the ratio between the Cramer–Rao bound and the actual variance. The estimator  W is called efficient, if \operatorname{eff}_{	heta}(W)=1 , i.e. if \mathop{\mathrm{Var}}
olimits_{	heta}(W)=g^{\prime}(	heta)^{2}/\mathcal{I}_% {n}(	heta) , for all 	heta\in\Theta . From proposition 13.3 we know that the efficiency is contained in the interval (0,1] , and since the bound is proportional to  1/n , an efficiency of  1/2 means that the estimator uses the information from n observations as well as an efficient estimator would use the information from  n/2 . An efficient estimator is a UMVUE in the sense of definition 10.3, because no unbiased estimator can be better than the bound, but the converse statement is not true, since the bound is not always attainable: in exercise 13.4, the UMVUE for the rate of the exponential distribution has efficiency (n-2)/n . These two results complement each other: the Lehmann–Scheffe theorem allows us to identify the best unbiased estimator, whereas the Cramer–Rao bound provides an absolute scale for comparing estimators. The proof tells us when the bound is attained: equality in the Cramer–Rao inequality is equality in the Cauchy–Schwarz step, and this only holds for random variables which are proportional to each other. Proposition 13.5. With the same assumptions as in proposition 13.3, the estimator  W is efficient if and only if there is a function c(	heta) such that equation (13.2) (13.2) W-g(	heta)=c(	heta)\,\ell^{\prime}(	heta)\qquad	ext{with probability one, % for every }	heta\in\Theta, and in this case c(	heta)=g^{\prime}(	heta)/\mathcal{I}_{n}(	heta) . In particular, if the model is a one-parameter exponential family as in definition 7.1 and the density is f(x;	heta)=h(x)\exp igl{(}\eta(	heta)T(x)-B(	heta) igr{)}, where \eta is continuously differentiable with \eta^{\prime}(	heta)
