Skip to content. toolbar Lecture 17 Asymptotic Normality and Efficiency of the MLE In lecture 16 we have seen that under regularity conditions the maximum likelihood estimator converges to the true parameter value. In this lecture we will discuss the error \hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0} in more detail: It transpires that for large sample size the error is approximately normally distributed with mean zero and variance given by the Cramer–Rao bound from lecture 13. Thus, the MLE is unbiased in the limit, and more importantly, is as accurate as any unbiased estimator can be. The proof of this result is a challenging one, but it is based on tools we have already learned in this module. The argument is based on Taylor expansion of the score function, and the resulting theorem allows us to compute standard errors and to derive approximate confidence statements. We will see the resulting numbers in lecture 18 and will make the statements precise in lecture 25. 17.1 The Theorem Using the notation from lecture 16, we write \mathcal{I}(	heta) for the Fisher information of a single observation and \mathcal{I}_{n}(	heta)=n\mathcal{I}(	heta) for the Fisher information of the sample, as given in definition 11.2 and proposition 11.4. The result is formulated in terms of the scaled error \sqrt{n}\,(\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0}) , as this is the only error term which has a non-degenerate limit: by consistency the error goes to zero and the factor \sqrt{n} is required to balance this effect. Theorem 17.1. Suppose that the regularity conditions (R1) to (R4) from definition 16.1 are satisfied, that \log igl{(}f(X_{1};	heta)/f(X_{1};	heta^{\prime}) igr{)} has finite mean under  \mathbb{P}_{	heta^{\prime}} for all 	heta,	heta^{\prime}\in\Theta , and that for every sample size n\geq 1 and every sample where all coordinates are in the support of the model, the likelihood equation \ell^{\prime}(	heta)=0 has a unique solution \hat{	heta}_{n} which coincides with the maximum likelihood estimator. Then \sqrt{n}\,(\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0})\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{d}\ }N\Bigl{(}0,%  rac{1}{\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0})}\Bigr{)}\qquad(n	o\infty). Before we give the proof, we unpack the statement of the theorem. The theorem informally states that, as  n gets large, \hat{	heta}_{n}\approx N\Bigl{(}	heta_{0}, rac{1}{n\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0})}% \Bigr{)}=N\Bigl{(}	heta_{0}, rac{1}{\mathcal{I}_{n}(	heta_{0})}\Bigr{)}. This statement contains three different aspects: (1) The centre of the approximating distribution equals the true value, i.e. the MLE is asymptotically unbiased (this does not mean that the MLE is unbiased for finite  n , only that it is asymptotically unbiased). (2) The spread of the distribution decreases as 1/\sqrt{n} , as for the sample mean. (3) The variance of the approximating distribution equals 1/\mathcal{I}_{n}(	heta_{0}) . This is the Cramer–Rao lower bound from lecture 13: no unbiased estimator can have smaller variance for any  n and the MLE achieves this bound in the limit. The last property has a name. Definition 17.2. An estimator 	ilde{	heta}_{n} for  	heta , which does not have to be the maximum likelihood estimator, is asymptotically efficient, if \sqrt{n}\,(	ilde{	heta}_{n}-	heta)\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{d}\ }N igl{(}0,1/% \mathcal{I}(	heta) igr{)} for all 	heta\in\Theta . In this language theorem 17.1 states that under the regularity conditions the MLE is asymptotically efficient. In lecture 13 we have seen that an efficient estimator, one which achieves the Cramer–Rao bound exactly, only exists for special models and parametrisations, namely the natural statistic of an exponential family. The asymptotic statement is much wider in applicability, as it holds for every regular model and every parametrisation, since by proposition 5.6 the MLE transforms together with the parameter. 17.2 Proof of the Theorem We will use a Taylor expansion of the score function \ell^{\prime} around the true value. Since the MLE is a root of the score, we can use Taylor expansion of \ell^{\prime}(\hat{	heta}_{n}) around the point 	heta_{0} to write the error term \hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0} in terms of \ell^{\prime}(	heta_{0}) and \ell^{\prime\prime}(	heta_{0}) , which are both sums of i.i.d. terms and thus can be analysed using the limit theorems for sample means. Proof of theorem 17.1. Let the true value be  	heta_{0} . Then, by (R3), the function \ell^{\prime} is two times continuously differentiable. Thus, by Taylor’s theorem with the Lagrange form of the remainder, for \ell^{\prime} around the point  	heta_{0} we get \ell^{\prime}(\hat{	heta}_{n})=\ell^{\prime}(	heta_{0})+\ell^{\prime\prime}(% 	heta_{0})\,(\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0})+	frac{1}{2}\,\ell^{\prime\prime% \prime}(	heta_{n}^{*})\,(\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0})^{2}, where 	heta_{n}^{*} is a point between 	heta_{0} and  \hat{	heta}_{n} . Since \hat{	heta}_{n} solves the likelihood equation, the left-hand side is zero. Solving for \hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0} and multiplying by \sqrt{n} we can write the scaled error as a ratio: equation (17.1) (17.1) \sqrt{n}\,(\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0})= rac{A_{n}}{B_{n}-C_{n}}, where A_{n}= rac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\,\ell^{\prime}(	heta_{0}),\qquad B_{n}=- rac{1}{n}% \,\ell^{\prime\prime}(	heta_{0}),\qquad C_{n}= rac{1}{2n}\,\ell^{\prime% \prime\prime}(	heta_{n}^{*})\,(\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0}). We now take the limit of each of the three terms separately. The numerator A_{n} is a scaled sum of i.i.d. terms: by definition of the score we have \ell^{\prime}(	heta_{0})=\sum_{i}\ell_{i}^{\prime}(	heta_{0}) and using the identities (16.1) we find that each term has mean zero and finite, positive variance  \mathcal{I}(	heta_{0}) . Thus, by the central limit theorem, theorem A.15, for the sample mean of the \ell_{i}^{\prime}(	heta_{0}) we get A_{n}=\sqrt{n}\,\Bigl{(} rac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\ell_{i}^{\prime}(	heta_{0})% -0\Bigr{)}\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{d}\ }N igl{(}0,\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0}) igr{% )}. The first term in the denominator is also a sample mean: B_{n}=- rac{1}{n}\sum_{i}\ell_{i}^{\prime\prime}(	heta_{0}) and by the law of large numbers, proposition A.14, and the second identity in (16.1) we find B_{n}\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{p}\ }-\mathbb{E}_{	heta_{0}} igl{(}\ell_{1}^{% \prime\prime}(	heta_{0}) igr{)}=\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0}). The remainder term C_{n} is where the consistency from lecture 16 and condition (R4) come into play. Let \delta and M be as in (R4). On the event \{|\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0}|<\delta\} , the intermediate point 	heta_{n}^{*} also lies within  \delta of  	heta_{0} . Thus, writing \ell^{\prime\prime\prime}(	heta_{n}^{*})=\sum_{i}\ell_{i}^{\prime\prime\prime% }(	heta_{n}^{*}) as a sum of the observations and using (R4) for each term, we find \Bigl{|} rac{1}{n}\,\ell^{\prime\prime\prime}(	heta_{n}^{*})\Bigr{|}\leq%  rac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n} igl{|}\ell_{i}^{\prime\prime\prime}(	heta_{n}^{*})%  igr{|}\leq rac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}M(X_{i}) on this event. By theorem 16.3, the probability of the event goes to one, and by the law of large numbers the right-hand side converges in probability to the finite constant \mathbb{E}_{	heta_{0}} igl{(}M(X_{1}) igr{)} . Thus, for all sufficiently large K we have \mathbb{P}_{	heta_{0}} igl{(}|\ell^{\prime\prime\prime}(	heta_{n}^{*})|/n>K%  igr{)}	o 0 . The other factor of C_{n} is \hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0} , which converges in probability to zero by theorem 16.3. For any \eta>0 , the event |C_{n}|>\eta is contained in the union of the events |\ell^{\prime\prime\prime}(	heta_{n}^{*})|/n>K and |\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0}|>2\eta/K , both of which have probability that goes to zero, and thus C_{n}\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{p}\ }0 . It remains to combine these three limits, and the appropriate tool for this is Slutsky’s theorem, proposition A.16. Since B_{n}\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{p}\ }\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0}) and C_{n}\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{p}\ }0 , the denominator satisfies B_{n}-C_{n}\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{p}\ }\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0}) , where the value is constant and non-zero. By the quotient part of Slutsky’s theorem, for the representation (17.1), we have \sqrt{n}\,(\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0})= rac{A_{n}}{B_{n}-C_{n}}\xrightarrow{% \ \mathrm{d}\ } rac{N igl{(}0,\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0}) igr{)}}{\mathcal{I}(% 	heta_{0})}=N\Bigl{(}0, rac{1}{\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0})}\Bigr{)}, where in the last step we used the fact that dividing a N(0,\sigma^{2}) random variable by the constant c>0 gives a N(0,\sigma^{2}/c^{2}) random variable, in this case with \sigma^{2}=\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0}) and c=\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0}) . This completes the proof. ∎ The structure of this argument is typical of most large-sample results in statistics: Taylor expansion is used to transform a question about an implicitly defined estimator into a question about sums of i.i.d. terms, the central limit theorem is used to analyse the leading term, the law of large numbers is used to analyse the coefficient of the leading term, the remainder is shown to be negligible using consistency of the estimator, and Slutsky’s theorem is used to combine the individual results. The only tricky step is the analysis of the remainder term, and condition (R4) is the reason that this term is straightforward to analyse. 17.3 Standard Errors The variance 1/\mathcal{I}_{n}(	heta_{0}) in theorem 17.1 depends on the unknown  	heta_{0} , and so cannot be reported as it stands. In practice we replace 	heta_{0} by its estimate, and the next result says that this does not disturb the limit. Corollary 17.3. With the same assumptions as in theorem 17.1, we have \sqrt{n\mathcal{I}(\hat{	heta}_{n})}\,(\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0})% \xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{d}\ }N(0,1)\qquad(n	o\infty). Proof. By (R3) the function \mathcal{I} is continuous and positive on  \Theta and thus the function 	heta\mapsto\sqrt{\mathcal{I}(	heta)/\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0})} is continuous at  	heta_{0} . By \hat{	heta}_{n}\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{p}\ }	heta_{0} from theorem 16.3, the continuous mapping theorem from proposition A.17 implies that \sqrt{\mathcal{I}(\hat{	heta}_{n})/\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0})}\xrightarrow{\ % \mathrm{p}\ }1 . By multiplying the result of theorem 17.1 by this factor and using the product part of Slutsky’s theorem, proposition A.16, we find \sqrt{n\mathcal{I}(\hat{	heta}_{n})}\,(\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0})=\sqrt{%  rac{\mathcal{I}(\hat{	heta}_{n})}{\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0})}}\cdot\sqrt{n% \mathcal{I}(	heta_{0})}\,(\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0})\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{% d}\ }1\cdot N(0,1), and the claim is proved. ∎ The quantity \mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}_{n})= rac{1}{\sqrt{\mathcal{I}_{n}% (\hat{	heta}_{n})}}= rac{1}{\sqrt{n\mathcal{I}(\hat{	heta}_{n})}} is called the standard error of the MLE. As a statistic, this quantity from the corollary is an approximation to the standard deviation of  \hat{	heta}_{n} for large  n . The corollary also allows us to justify approximate confidence statements: Since a standard normal variable takes values in (-1.96,1.96) with probability  0.95 , the random interval \hat{	heta}_{n}\pm 1.96\,\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}_{n}) covers  	heta_{0} with probability which tends to  0.95 . Intervals of this form were used in an informal way in lecture 12 and we will make these intervals precise in lecture 25. In lecture 18 we will use simulation to check how close the real coverage of these intervals is to  0.95 for moderate  n . If we do not have a closed formula for \mathcal{I} , the denominator \mathcal{I}_{n}(\hat{	heta}_{n}) can be replaced by the observed curvature -\ell^{\prime\prime}(\hat{	heta}_{n}) of the log-likelihood at the maximum. This approach is taken by a numerical optimiser and we will use the resulting values in lecture 18. 17.4 Examples For the MLEs which are sample means, the Bernoulli and Poisson MLEs from lecture 5, theorem 17.1 does not give any new results: the central limit theorem allows us to find the asymptotic normality of these estimators directly and from exercises 17.1 and 17.2 we know that the two variances are the same. For the normal mean with known variance, the MLE   ar{X} is exactly normally distributed for every  n , with variance \sigma^{2}/n=1/\mathcal{I}_{n}(	heta) . Thus, for this case the theorem gives an exact result. The first new case is an MLE which is not a sample mean. Example 17.4. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. with exponential rate  	heta . Then the MLE for the parameter value is \hat{	heta}_{n}=1/ ar{X} from example 5.2 and by exercise 16.1 the regularity conditions and the uniqueness of the root hold. From example 11.8 we know that the Fisher information is given by \mathcal{I}(	heta)=1/	heta^{2} . Thus, by theorem 17.1, \sqrt{n}\,\Bigl{(} rac{1}{ ar{X}}-	heta\Bigr{)}\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{d}\ }% N(0,	heta^{2}), with standard error \mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}_{n})=\hat{	heta}_{n}/\sqrt{n} . We can compare the result to exact results as follows: In lecture 5 we found \mathbb{E}_{	heta}(\hat{	heta}_{n})=n	heta/(n-1) , which is a bias of 	heta/(n-1) . In exercise 5.3 we found the variance \mathop{\mathrm{Var}}
olimits_{	heta}(\hat{	heta}_{n})=n^{2}	heta^{2}/%  igl{(}(n-1)^{2}(n-2) igr{)} . If we multiply this variance by  n , it converges to  	heta^{2} . If we multiply the bias by  \sqrt{n} , it converges to zero, as predicted by the theorem: for large  n , the bias is of a smaller order than the spread of the distribution, and thus disappears in the limit. The theorem can also be applied to functions of the parameter. The mean of the exponential distribution is \mu=g(	heta)=1/	heta and by proposition 5.6 the MLE for this parameter is \hat{\mu}_{n}=1/\hat{	heta}_{n}= ar{X} . Using the delta method, proposition A.18 with g^{\prime}(	heta)=-1/	heta^{2} , we get \sqrt{n}\,( ar{X}-\mu)\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{d}\ }N igl{(}0,\ g^{\prime}(% 	heta)^{2}\,	heta^{2} igr{)}=N\Bigl{(}0, rac{1}{	heta^{2}}\Bigr{)}=N(0,% \mu^{2}). This is the central limit theorem for   ar{X} , since the exponential distribution has variance 1/	heta^{2}=\mu^{2} . The standard error of   ar{X} ,  ar{X}/\sqrt{n} , is the standard error we would obtain by computing the standard error from first principles. The example illustrates a general point: for any smooth function g(	heta) of the parameter, proposition 5.6 gives the MLE g(\hat{	heta}_{n}) , and the delta method gives the asymptotic distribution and standard error |g^{\prime}(\hat{	heta}_{n})|\,\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}_{n}) , without any further maximisation. 17.5 Quality of the Approximation Theorem 17.1 is a limit statement and does not give information about the required sample size for the normal approximation to be applicable. The required value of n for the normal approximation to be usable depends on the model and there are three situations where care is needed. First, for small samples from a model with a skewed distribution: for the exponential rate, \hat{	heta}_{n}=1/ ar{X} has a right-skewed distribution and a bias of 	heta/(n-1) which is not captured in the limit but which is visible for n around  10 . Secondly, when a parameter value is close to the boundary of the parameter space: for the Bernoulli distribution with 	heta_{0} close to zero or one, the standard error \sqrt{\hat{	heta}_{n}(1-\hat{	heta}_{n})/n} is zero whenever all observations have the same value, and thus the interval \hat{	heta}_{n}\pm 1.96\,\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}_{n}) has zero width in this case. Finally, if the regularity conditions are violated: for the uniform distribution on  (0,	heta) , exercise 17.3 shows that the error of the MLE is of order 1/n instead of 1/\sqrt{n} , and that the limiting distribution is exponential instead of normal, as expected from the super-efficiency of the parameter estimates as shown in lecture 13. The first case is investigated by simulation in lecture 12, by checking the coverage of the interval \hat{	heta}_{n}\pm 1.96\,\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}_{n}) for moderate  n . Finally, the theorem can be extended to a vector parameter 	heta=(	heta_{1},\dots,	heta_{k}) : Under appropriate regularity conditions, the MLE is asymptotically normal with mean  	heta_{0} and covariance matrix \mathcal{I}_{n}(	heta_{0})^{-1} , the inverse of the Fisher information matrix from lecture 14. Without proof, the argument is similar to the one above, but uses Taylor expansion for several variables. For the normal distribution with unknown parameters, where the information matrix is diagonal, this gives the asymptotic normality of  ar{X} and of the variance estimator separately. Summary. •​Under regularity conditions and assuming the uniqueness of the likelihood root, we have \sqrt{n}\,(\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta_{0})\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{d}\ }N igl{(}0,% 1/\mathcal{I}(	heta_{0}) igr{)} : the MLE is asymptotically unbiased, the error is of order 1/\sqrt{n} , and the asymptotic variance equals the Cramer–Rao bound, i.e. the MLE is asymptotically efficient. •​The proof uses Taylor expansion of the score around  	heta_{0} , central limit theorem for the score, law of large numbers for the second derivative, consistency and (R4) for the remainder, and Slutsky’s theorem to combine these results. •​Replacing 	heta_{0} by \hat{	heta}_{n} in the expression for the variance does not change the limit and thus we get the standard error \mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}_{n})=1/\sqrt{\mathcal{I}_{n}(\hat{% 	heta}_{n})} and the approximate 95\% confidence interval \hat{	heta}_{n}\pm 1.96\,\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}_{n}) for the parameter value. •​Using the delta method, we can apply the result to any smooth function of the parameter. The standard error for such an application is |g^{\prime}(\hat{	heta}_{n})|\,\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}_{n}) . •​The approximation can be poor for small  n in models with skewed distributions and at the boundary of the parameter space, and it breaks down completely when the regularity conditions are violated, e.g. for the uniform distribution. Exercise 17.1. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. Bernoulli with success probability  	heta\in(0,1) , where the MLE \hat{	heta}_{n}= ar{X} and Fisher information \mathcal{I}(	heta)=1/ igl{(}	heta(1-	heta) igr{)} are given in example 11.5. 1.​State the result of theorem 17.1 for this model and verify that it coincides with the central limit theorem for   ar{X} . 2.​The log-odds are given by \psi=g(	heta)=\log igl{(}	heta/(1-	heta) igr{)} . Find the MLE for  \psi and use the delta method to show that \sqrt{n}\,(\hat{\psi}_{n}-\psi)\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{d}\ }N igl{(}0,1/(% 	heta(1-	heta)) igr{)} . 3.​Determine the standard error of  \hat{\psi}_{n} and an approximate 95\% interval for  \psi , in terms of \hat{	heta}_{n} and  n . Exercise 17.2. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. Poisson with parameter  	heta>0 , where MLE \hat{	heta}_{n}= ar{X} and Fisher information \mathcal{I}(	heta)=1/	heta are given in example 11.6. 1.​State the result of theorem 17.1 for this model and verify that the result coincides with the central limit theorem. 2.​In exercise 5.7 we have found the MLE \hat{p}_{0}=e^{- ar{X}} for the probability p_{0}=\mathbb{P}_{	heta}(X_{1}=0)=e^{-	heta} and we have seen that this MLE is biased. Using the delta method, determine the asymptotic distribution of \sqrt{n}\,(\hat{p}_{0}-p_{0}) . Why does the bias, found in the previous step, not contradict the result from this question? Exercise 17.3. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. uniformly distributed on the interval  (0,	heta) . The MLE for this model is \hat{	heta}_{n}=\max_{i}X_{i} . 1.​For every x\geq 0 , show that \mathbb{P}_{	heta} igl{(}n(	heta-\hat{	heta}_{n})>x igr{)}	o e^{-x/	heta} as n	o\infty , i.e. that n(	heta-\hat{	heta}_{n}) converges in distribution to the exponential distribution with mean  	heta , i.e. with rate  1/	heta . 2.​Show that \sqrt{n}\,(\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta)\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{p}\ }0 . Why is the MLE not asymptotically normal in the sense of theorem 17.1? Why does this make sense in the context of the super-efficiency of the uniform model from lecture 13? Previous page Next page

Skip to content. toolbar Lecture 18 Maximum Likelihood in R This is the R session for week 6. In lectures 16 and 17 we have been concentrating on some mathematical proofs, and in this week we will be considering the resulting theorems in a numerical context: we will find the maximum likelihood estimate for a model where the likelihood equation cannot be solved explicitly, we will learn how to read off the standard errors from the output of the optimiser, and we will use simulation to determine how often the interval \hat{	heta}\pm 1.96\,\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}) from corollary 17.3 contains the truth. The written reference for this session is again appendix B. 18.1 The Log-Likelihood as an R Function The model of the day is X_{1},\dots,X_{n} i.i.d. from the gamma distribution with shape  \alpha and rate   eta , both unknown. Example 4.9 gave the method of moments estimates 	ilde{\alpha}= ar{X}^{2}/V and 	ilde{ eta}= ar{X}/V , with V the plug-in variance, and section 5.3 noted that the likelihood equations have no closed-form solution. We simulate one sample of size n=40 with \alpha=2.5 and  eta=0.5 . The command dgamma with log = TRUE can be used to evaluate the logarithm of the density at every observation, and the sum of these values is \ell(\alpha, eta) . Since the R optimisers minimise, the function returns the negative log-likelihood, and since they hand over all parameters as one vector, it takes the vector par, shape first and rate second. copy code alpha <- 2.5 beta <- 0.5 n <- 40 set.seed(2026) x <- rgamma(n, shape = alpha, rate = beta) nll <- function(par, data) { -sum(dgamma(data, shape = par[1], rate = par[2], log = TRUE)) } nll(c(2.5, 0.5), x) nll(c(1, 1), x) copy code ## [1] 102.0691 ## [1] 217.3799 The true parameter values lead to a much smaller negative log-likelihood than the pair (1,1) . Here we assume that we know the rate   eta=0.5 and that we want to estimate the shape parameter  \alpha . The command optimise can be used to find the minimum of a function of one variable in a given interval, without requiring a starting value. Before you try the command, make a sketch of the negative log-likelihood as a function of  \alpha on the interval from 0.1 to  20 . How many dips will there be? Will the minimum lie above or below  2.5 ? copy code optimise(function(a) nll(c(a, 0.5), x), interval = c(0.1, 20)) copy code ## $minimum ## [1] 2.624051 ## ## $objective ## [1] 101.924 The estimate \hat{\alpha}=2.62 is a little above the true value, and the minimum 101.9 lies below the value 102.1 where the truth is located. Since both parameters are now unknown, we have to use optim, which minimises a function of a vector, and which requires a starting value. From section 16.4 we know that a good way to find a starting value is to use a simple, consistent estimator. One possible choice for this is the method of moments estimate. Before you try the code, predict whether the maximum likelihood estimates will agree with the method of moments estimates to one decimal place. copy code V <- mean((x - mean(x))^2) start <- c(mean(x)^2 / V, mean(x) / V) start fit <- optim(start, nll, data = x) fit$par fit$convergence fit$counts copy code ## [1] 2.2942540 0.4221649 ## [1] 2.258769 0.415672 ## [1] 0 ## function gradient ## 43 NA The extra argument data = x is passed on to our function. The result fit is a list: fit$par holds the estimates, \hat{\alpha}=2.26 and \hat{ eta}=0.42 , which agree with the start to one decimal place; fit$convergence is 0 if the search finished properly, and any other value means the estimates cannot be trusted; fit$counts records that 43 evaluations were needed. By default optim uses the Nelder–Mead method, which needs no derivatives; appendix B shows the alternative "BFGS". 18.2 Standard Errors from the Observed Information From theorem 17.1 and the vector version of this result from lecture 17 we know that the MLE is approximately normally distributed with covariance matrix \mathcal{I}_{n}(	heta_{0})^{-1} . As in section 17.3, we can replace \mathcal{I}_{n}(\hat{	heta}) by the observed information, i.e. the matrix of second derivatives of the negative log-likelihood at the maximum. The optim function can compute this matrix for us if we ask for it explicitly, using hessian = TRUE. The matrix can be inverted using the solve command, the diag command gives the diagonal of this matrix, and the square roots of the diagonal elements are the standard errors. Before you look at the output, predict which of the two standard errors will be larger and by how much. copy code fit <- optim(start, nll, data = x, hessian = TRUE) fit$hessian covmat <- solve(fit$hessian) se <- sqrt(diag(covmat)) result <- cbind(estimate = fit$par, se = se, lower = fit$par - 1.96 * se, upper = fit$par + 1.96 * se) rownames(result) <- c("shape", "rate") round(result, 3) cov2cor(covmat)[1, 2] copy code ## [,1] [,2] ## [1,] 22.18712 -96.2299 ## [2,] -96.22990 522.9200 ## estimate se lower upper ## shape 2.259 0.473 1.333 3.185 ## rate 0.416 0.097 0.225 0.606 ## [1] 0.8933916 The table prints the results of a maximum likelihood estimate in an easy to read format. Each row corresponds to one parameter, and shows the parameter estimate, the standard error and the interval \hat{	heta}\pm 1.96\,\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}) from corollary 17.3. Both intervals capture the true parameter values. The standard error for the shape parameter is approximately five times larger than the standard error for the rate parameter, but, since the estimates differ by a similar factor, the two standard errors are comparable relative to the estimates. The final row of the table, computed using the cov2cor function, shows that the two estimates have correlation 0.89 . It is easy to show that, if we increase the shape and the rate simultaneously, while keeping the mean \alpha/ eta fixed, the log-likelihood changes only slightly. Thus the data constrains the ratio \alpha/ eta well, but poorly constrains each parameter individually. This is the problem of a nuisance parameter as discussed in lecture 14. 18.3 Starting Values The gamma log-likelihood function has only one peak, so the method is forgiving. Before you try the code, make a prediction: what happens if we start with very different values, e.g. with shape 30 and rate  30 ? Will optim still find the maximum? Will it take more than 43 evaluations to find the maximum, when we used the “good” start? copy code fit_far <- optim(c(30, 30), nll, data = x) fit_far$par fit_far$counts copy code ## [1] 2.2571696 0.4154238 ## function gradient ## 97 NA The search reaches the same maximum, up to two decimal places, but it takes more than twice as long and R complains about several warnings and the presence of NaNs (not a number) in the output: the search tried to use negative parameter values where dgamma is not defined. We can prevent this by adding a line at the top of the function which returns Inf if any(par <= 0). This will prevent the optimiser from trying these values. For a “good” start this is not required. The real problem of a poor start is when the likelihood has several local maxima, as in section 5.3. We illustrate this effect using a toy function which is not a model: the logarithm of a two-component normal mixture density, evaluated at  	heta , has a local maximum at 	heta=2 and a lower local maximum at 	heta=-2 (see figure 18.1). Which of these two peaks will optimise report for the interval from -6 to  4 , which contains both maxima? Which optim result will you get if you start at -3 and at  3 , respectively? To find the maximum, optim needs to use a different method than the default method, and we can use the BFGS method. Also, we need to tell optimise that we want to find a maximum by using the argument maximum = TRUE. copy code toy <- function(theta) { log(0.7 * dnorm(theta, mean = 2) + 0.3 * dnorm(theta, mean = -2, sd = 0.7)) } curve(toy(x), from = -5, to = 5) optimise(toy, interval = c(-6, 4), maximum = TRUE)$maximum optim(-3, function(theta) -toy(theta), method = "BFGS")$par optim(3, function(theta) -toy(theta), method = "BFGS")$par copy code ## [1] -1.99892 ## [1] -1.998923 ## [1] 2 A smooth curve of theta ranging from -5 to 5, featuring two peaks: a smaller one around theta = -2 and a larger, broader one around theta = 2, with a dip around theta = -0.4. Figure 18.1: The toy function with the two peaks. The peak found by optim depends on the starting point. If optimise is used, it returns the lower peak, despite the interval containing both peaks. Both optimisers find a local maximum, but neither of them can determine whether this is the global maximum: optim starts at and ends at the first peak it encounters, and optimise assumes that there is only one peak. There is no command which allows us to fix this problem. The only way to avoid this problem is to use some theory and to follow certain habits: always start with a consistent estimator (as we did for the gamma distribution earlier in this lecture), always plot the log-likelihood function (if the number of parameters is one), and always start the optim procedure again to see whether the solution is different. 18.4 Coverage of the Wald Interval While corollary 17.3 shows that the interval \hat{	heta}\pm 1.96\,\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}) covers the truth with probability approaching  0.95 , in section 17.5 we have seen that sometimes, for moderate  n , this is not the case. To verify the claim for the gamma shape and rate, we use simulation in this section, following the approach to coverage introduced in lecture 12. The complete script for our simulation is given in the following box, with the lines shuffled (except for the bodies of the function and of the loop). Before you read the rest of this section, try to do the following: (1) write down the lines in a way which forms a working script, and (2) predict the coverages for n=20 : will they be above or below  0.95 ? Which of the two, shape or rate, will be worse? copy code data.frame(n = ns, shape = coverage[, 1], rate = coverage[, 2]) fit_gamma <- function(n, alpha, beta) { x <- rgamma(n, shape = alpha, rate = beta) V <- mean((x - mean(x))^2) start <- c(mean(x)^2 / V, mean(x) / V) fit <- optim(start, nll, data = x, hessian = TRUE) c(fit$par, sqrt(diag(solve(fit$hessian)))) } set.seed(2026) for (i in seq_along(ns)) { res <- replicate(5000, fit_gamma(ns[i], alpha, beta)) lower <- res[1:2, ] - 1.96 * res[3:4, ] upper <- res[1:2, ] + 1.96 * res[3:4, ] coverage[i, 1] <- mean(lower[1, ] <= alpha & alpha <= upper[1, ]) coverage[i, 2] <- mean(lower[2, ] <= beta & beta <= upper[2, ]) } alpha <- 2.5 coverage <- matrix(0, nrow = length(ns), ncol = 2) ns <- c(20, 100) beta <- 0.5 Every name must exist before it is used: the function and the four assignments come first, with coverage after ns; then the seed, then the loop, and the table last. The one new detail is the return value, the two estimates followed by the two standard errors, which replicate stacks as the columns of a matrix; thus res[1:2, ] holds the estimates and res[3:4, ] the standard errors. The script in working order is R/S18-coverage.R; it fits the model 5000 times for each sample size, takes a few seconds, and prints the following table. copy code ## n shape rate ## 1 20 0.9624 0.9604 ## 2 100 0.9508 0.9526 At n=100 both coverages are within simulation error of the nominal 0.95 , as expected from corollary 17.3. At n=20 both coverages are above the nominal level, around 0.96 : the interval is slightly too wide rather than too narrow. The total hides an imbalance again, and the script also counts for the shape at n=20 how many misses are below the truth and how many are above. copy code ## below above ## 0.0296 0.0116 Almost three quarters of the misses are below the true shape. Since the standard error increases with the estimate, an underestimated shape will result in a short interval which does not reach the truth, whereas an overestimated shape will result in a long interval which usually still covers the truth. The same mechanism was at work for the exponential rate in lecture 12. 18.5 A Task to Take Home The following task is in the style of a practical report. The published scripts of this lecture can be used as a starting point. Use your student ID to seed the random number generator, and choose your own shape and rate. Simulate one gamma sample of size n=30 . Use optim to fit the model by maximum likelihood, starting with estimates from the method of moments. For each parameter, report the estimate, the standard error, and the interval \hat{	heta}\pm 1.96\,\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}) , in three separate sentences. For each interval, state whether the interval contains the value you chose. Estimate the coverage of both intervals when n=30 , using 2000 replicates. Write a short paragraph about whether you would trust the intervals for this sample size. Finally, fit the normal model with unknown mean and variance, using optim, to a normal sample of size  30 . Check that the estimates match the closed forms from example 5.4, and that the standard error of the mean matches \hat{\sigma}/\sqrt{n} . Before the next session, you should type the commands used in this session, and should check that you get the same results as the text. Summary. •​The written documentation for optim, optimise, solve and the Hessian matrix can be found in appendix B. •​The negative log-likelihood can be represented as an R function for one parameter vector, using the density with log = TRUE. optimise can then be used to minimise this function over an interval for one parameter, and optim can be used to find the minimum for several parameters, starting from a given point. It is important to check fit$convergence to see whether the optimisation was successful. •​If hessian = TRUE, the optimiser returns the observed information matrix. The inverse of this matrix can be used to approximately determine the covariance matrix of the MLE, and the square roots of the diagonal elements can be used to determine the standard errors reported in section 17.3. The results of a fit can be printed in a table which shows the estimates, standard errors and intervals for each parameter. •​A good starting point for the optimisation procedure is to use a consistent estimator, e.g. based on the method of moments. Also, since an optimiser finds a local maximum, it is important to plot the log-likelihood and to re-run the optimisation procedure with a different starting point. •​For the gamma distribution, with shape and rate parameters, the coverage of the Wald interval is slightly above 95\% for n=20 and close to 95\% for n=100 , with most of the misses being below the true value. This is caused by small estimates having small standard errors. Previous page Next page

Skip to content. toolbar Lecture 2 Bias, Mean Squared Error and Consistency We finished the previous lecture with the idea that a good estimator is one which has a sampling distribution concentrated around the true value  	heta_{0} , for all possible true values. In this lecture we quantify this idea: the bias measures whether the estimator is on average right, the mean squared error measures how far away the estimator typically is from the truth, and consistency is the property that the estimator converges to the truth as the amount of data increases. The main idea to take away is the decomposition of the mean squared error into a variance and a squared bias. 2.1 Bias and Unbiasedness The most basic question we can ask about an estimator is whether it gives the correct answer on average. Definition 2.1. The bias of an estimator \hat{	heta} for  	heta is given by \mathop{\mathrm{bias}}
