Skip to content. toolbar Lecture 25 Confidence Intervals So far we have considered estimators which output a single number. In this lecture we will consider intervals of parameter values, constructed such that the true parameter value lies inside the interval with a given probability. We will see two different ways to construct such an interval, one using the concept of a pivotal quantity and the other one based on the inversion of a family of hypothesis tests. This latter approach also allows us to relate the testing theory from lectures 20 to 23 to the topic of estimation. The Bayesian equivalents of confidence intervals are called credible intervals and we will learn about these in lecture 28. In lecture 27 we will compare the two approaches to confidence intervals using simulation. 25.1 Definition and Interpretation Here, X=(X_{1},\dots,X_{n}) is a sample from a parametric model with parameter 	heta\in\Theta\subseteq\mathbb{R} , and \mathbb{P}_{	heta} is the distribution of the sample under  	heta . For a fixed small \alpha\in(0,1) , typically \alpha=0.05 , the aim is to find an interval which misses the truth with probability at most  \alpha . Definition 25.1. Let L(X) and U(X) be statistics such that L(X)\leq U(X) . The random interval [L(X),U(X)] is a confidence interval for  	heta with confidence level 1-\alpha , if \mathbb{P}_{	heta} igl{(}L(X)\leq	heta\leq U(X) igr{)}\geq 1-\alpha\qquad% 	ext{for all }	heta\in\Theta. The probability on the left-hand side is called the coverage probability of the interval at  	heta . The endpoints of the interval are statistics, i.e. functions of the data, while the parameter  	heta is an unknown constant which does not change between samples. For continuous data we can usually achieve equality in the definition, but for discrete data the coverage probability of the interval can jump as 	heta changes. For this reason, the definition only requires the left-hand side to satisfy the inequality. More generally, any random subset C(X)\subseteq\Theta of the parameter space such that \mathbb{P}_{	heta} igl{(}	heta\in C(X) igr{)}\geq 1-\alpha for all  	heta is called a confidence set. Some care is needed when interpreting a confidence interval. For example, if the data lead to the 95\% interval [9.32,11.28] , both endpoints are now fixed numbers, and so is the unknown  	heta ; the statement 9.32\leq	heta\leq 11.28 is either true or false. The 95\% describes the procedure: 95\% of the computed intervals would cover the truth in repeated samples, but we do not know whether the interval in front of us is one of these. The statement “ 	heta is in [9.32,11.28] with probability  0.95 ” is wrong, since it assumes that the parameter is random (this only holds in the Bayesian model from lecture 28). The statement “ 95\% of the observations are in the interval” is wrong, since this would be a confusion of parameter with data. The correct interpretation of such an interval can be illustrated by simulating many intervals and seeing how many of these cover the truth. This is done in lecture 27. 25.2 Pivotal Quantities The most direct approach to constructing a confidence interval is to construct an interval based on a quantity whose distribution is known exactly, regardless of the parameter value. Such a quantity is called a pivotal quantity. Definition 25.2. A function Q(X,	heta) of the sample and the parameter is a pivotal quantity or pivot, if the distribution of Q(X,	heta) under \mathbb{P}_{	heta} does not depend on 	heta\in\Theta . A pivot is not a statistic, because it contains the unknown  	heta , but we know the quantiles of the pivot distribution. To get a confidence interval, we can choose constants a<b such that \mathbb{P}_{	heta} igl{(}a\leq Q(X,	heta)\leq b igr{)}=1-\alpha and then solve the inequalities a\leq Q(X,	heta)\leq b for  	heta . If Q is monotone in  	heta , the solution of these inequalities forms an interval which, with probability exactly 1-\alpha , contains the unknown parameter value. Using the quantile notation  z_{p} from lecture 20, we have z_{0.975}=1.96 and, using symmetry, z_{1-p}=-z_{p} . Similarly, we write t_{k,p} and \chi^{2}_{k,p} for the p -quantiles of the t_{k} and the \chi^{2}_{k} distribution, respectively. These quantiles are all tabulated. Example 25.3. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d.  N(	heta,\sigma^{2}) where \sigma^{2} is known. Then, by proposition A.4,  ar{X}\sim N(	heta,\sigma^{2}/n) and thus Q(X,	heta)= rac{\sqrt{n}\,( ar{X}-	heta)}{\sigma}\sim N(0,1) for all  	heta . Thus, Q is a pivot. We can find a confidence interval for  	heta by bracketing Q between -z_{1-\alpha/2} and z_{1-\alpha/2} and then solving the resulting inequalities for  	heta . The resulting confidence interval is \Bigl{[}\, ar{X}-z_{1-\alpha/2}\, rac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}},\  ar{X}+z_{1-% \alpha/2}\, rac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}\,\Bigr{]} with level 1-\alpha . For n=16 observations, with \sigma=2 and  ar{x}=10.3 , the 95\% interval is 10.3\pm 1.96\cdot 0.5=[9.32,11.28] . This is the same interval as we discussed above. If \sigma^{2} is unknown, this pivot is not available. If we replace \sigma by the sample standard deviation  S , we get a new pivot and we have to consider the t distribution from appendix A. Example 25.4. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d.  N(\mu,\sigma^{2}) , where both parameters are unknown, and let S^{2} be the sample variance. By proposition A.4, the two values Z= rac{\sqrt{n}\,( ar{X}-\mu)}{\sigma}\sim N(0,1)\qquad	ext{and}\qquad V=%  rac{(n-1)S^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}\sim\chi^{2}_{n-1} are independent. By definition A.5, the ratio T= rac{Z}{\sqrt{V/(n-1)}}= rac{\sqrt{n}\,( ar{X}-\mu)}{S} is distributed as t_{n-1} and, since the unknown  \sigma cancels, T is a pivot for  \mu , independently of the nuisance parameter  \sigma^{2} . The t -interval \Bigl{[}\, ar{X}-t_{n-1,1-\alpha/2}\, rac{S}{\sqrt{n}},\  ar{X}+t_{n-1,1-% \alpha/2}\, rac{S}{\sqrt{n}}\,\Bigr{]}, with level 1-\alpha , can be obtained by bracketing the pivot as above. For the data from example 25.3, with sample standard deviation s=2.1 , we can use the table to find t_{15,0.975}=2.131 and the 95\% interval 10.3\pm 2.131\cdot 2.1/4=[9.18,11.42] . The wider interval is the price we pay for not knowing  \sigma . As n increases, this difference decreases, since t_{n-1} converges to the standard normal distribution. Example 25.5. In the same setting, V=(n-1)S^{2}/\sigma^{2}\sim\chi^{2}_{n-1} is a pivot for  \sigma^{2} , independently of the unknown  \mu . If we bracket this value between \chi^{2}_{n-1,\alpha/2} and \chi^{2}_{n-1,1-\alpha/2} and then solve for  \sigma^{2} , the order of the endpoints is reversed, and we obtain the confidence interval \Bigl{[}\, rac{(n-1)S^{2}}{\chi^{2}_{n-1,1-\alpha/2}},\  rac{(n-1)S^{2}}{% \chi^{2}_{n-1,\alpha/2}}\,\Bigr{]} for  \sigma^{2} with level 1-\alpha . For n=16 and s^{2}=4.41 the table gives \chi^{2}_{15,0.025}=6.262 and \chi^{2}_{15,0.975}=27.488 , and thus the 95\% interval is given by [66.15/27.488,\ 66.15/6.262]=[2.41,10.56]. This interval is far from symmetric around the estimate  4.41 . If we take the square root of both endpoints, the interval [1.55,3.25] for  \sigma has the same coverage probability: applying a strictly increasing function to both endpoints does not change the coverage probability, whereas applying a decreasing function swaps the endpoints. 25.3 Inverting a Test A level- \alpha test of H_{0}\colon	heta=	heta_{0} decides whether a single value  	heta_{0} is compatible with the data; a confidence interval collects all the values which are. The following lemma, using the notions of critical region and level from definitions 20.2 and 20.5, makes this correspondence exact, in both directions. Lemma 25.6. For every 	heta_{0}\in\Theta , let A(	heta_{0}) be the acceptance region of a level- \alpha test of H_{0}\colon	heta=	heta_{0} , that is the set of samples for which H_{0} is not rejected. Then C(X)= igl{\{}	heta_{0}\in\Theta\mathrel{ ig{|}}X\in A(	heta_{0}) igr{\}}, is a confidence set for  	heta with level 1-\alpha . Conversely, if C(X) is a confidence set with level 1-\alpha , then for each 	heta_{0}\in\Theta the test which rejects H_{0}\colon	heta=	heta_{0} if and only if 	heta_{0}
otin C(X) has level  \alpha . Proof. By the definition of  C(X) , for every 	heta_{0} and every sample we have 	heta_{0}\in C(X) if and only if X\in A(	heta_{0}) . Let 	heta be the true value. Then, for 	heta_{0}=	heta , under \mathbb{P}_{	heta} the null hypothesis H_{0}\colon	heta_{0}=	heta is true and the test with acceptance region A(	heta) rejects with probability at most  \alpha . We have \mathbb{P}_{	heta} igl{(}	heta\in C(X) igr{)}=\mathbb{P}_{	heta} igl{(}X% \in A(	heta) igr{)}=1-\mathbb{P}_{	heta} igl{(}X
otin A(	heta) igr{)}% \geq 1-\alpha. Since 	heta was arbitrary, C(X) is a confidence set with level  1-\alpha . For the converse statement, the test for H_{0}\colon	heta=	heta_{0} rejects if and only if 	heta_{0}
otin C(X) . Thus, under \mathbb{P}_{	heta_{0}} , this event has probability 1-\mathbb{P}_{	heta_{0}} igl{(}	heta_{0}\in C(X) igr{)}\leq\alpha and the test has level  \alpha . This completes the proof. ∎ The lemma is a change of viewpoint, rather than a new result: the event “ 	heta_{0} is not rejected by  X ” can be read both as a test (with 	heta_{0} fixed and X random) and as a confidence set (with X fixed and 	heta_{0} varying). As an illustration, the size- \alpha two-sided test from example 20.7 for the mean of an i.i.d.  N(	heta,\sigma^{2}) sample with known  \sigma^{2} rejects H_{0}\colon	heta=	heta_{0} if | ar{X}-	heta_{0}|>z_{1-\alpha/2}\,\sigma/\sqrt{n} and the values not rejected by the test form the interval from example 25.3. Similarly, by inverting the one-sided test from example 20.6, we find a one-sided confidence interval. We will consider this type of interval again in section 25.5. Finally, in exercise 25.4, the same approach is repeated with unknown  \sigma . In practice, the duality is most often used in the opposite direction: a value  	heta_{0} is rejected at level  \alpha if it does not belong to the 1-\alpha interval. 25.4 Approximate Intervals from the MLE Exact pivots only exist for special models. For every regular model, however, corollary 17.3 provides an approximate pivot: the standardised error (\hat{	heta}_{n}-	heta)/\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}_{n}) of the maximum likelihood estimator, with \mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}_{n})=1/\sqrt{\mathcal{I}_{n}(\hat{% 	heta}_{n})} , converges in distribution to N(0,1) whatever the value of  	heta . Proposition 25.7. With the same assumptions as in corollary 17.3, the Wald interval  igl{[}\,\hat{	heta}_{n}-z_{1-\alpha/2}\,\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{% 	heta}_{n}),\ \hat{	heta}_{n}+z_{1-\alpha/2}\,\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
