olimits(\hat{	heta}_{n}) for any smooth function g(	heta) (see exercise 25.3). However, the coverage of this interval is only guaranteed in the limit, and section 17.5 lists the cases to be aware of. Example 25.8. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. Bernoulli with success probability 	heta\in(0,1) . The MLE is \hat{	heta}_{n}= ar{X} by example 5.3 and the Fisher information is \mathcal{I}(	heta)=1/ igl{(}	heta(1-	heta) igr{)} by example 11.5. Thus the Wald interval is  ar{X}\pm z_{1-\alpha/2}\sqrt{ ar{X}(1- ar{X})/n} . For 37 successes in n=100 trials we have  ar{x}=0.37 , the standard error is \sqrt{0.37\cdot 0.63/100}=0.048 , and the 95\% interval is 0.37\pm 1.96\cdot 0.048=[0.275,0.465] . If 	heta is close to 0 or  1 , or if n is small, the coverage of this interval may be less than 1-\alpha , since the normal approximation to   ar{X} is poor in these cases and the standard error equals zero if all observations are the same. 25.5 One-Sided Intervals and the Choice of Interval Sometimes it is only important to consider one direction of a confidence interval, for example when we want to show that a failure rate is less than or equal to some value. A lower confidence bound for  	heta with level 1-\alpha is a statistic  L(X) such that \mathbb{P}_{	heta} igl{(}L(X)\leq	heta igr{)}\geq 1-\alpha for all  	heta . The corresponding one-sided confidence interval is then [L(X),\infty) . An upper confidence bound is defined symmetrically. In the pivot method, a one-sided interval can be obtained by considering the single inequality Q(X,	heta)\leq b , where b is the (1-\alpha) -quantile of the pivot. For example, for the normal distribution with known variance, this gives the lower bound  ar{X}-z_{1-\alpha}\,\sigma/\sqrt{n} as promised in section 25.3, where z_{1-\alpha} is used instead of z_{1-\alpha/2} since all of the error probability is allocated to one side. In the context of the pivot method, any pair of values a<b with \mathbb{P}(a\leq Q\leq b)=1-\alpha defines a valid two-sided interval. Here we use the equal-tailed choice, i.e.  a and b are the (\alpha/2) - and (1-\alpha/2) -quantiles, respectively. This choice is the shortest interval for a symmetric, unimodal pivot density, but may not be shortest for skewed densities like the chi-squared distribution; the interval boundaries can be found in the tables. Exercise 25.2 shows an example where the shortest interval is one-sided. In lecture 28 we will see the same choice of interval again, when we consider credible intervals. Summary. •​A confidence interval covers the true parameter with probability at least 1-\alpha for all  	heta . The interval is random, the parameter is fixed, and the level describes the procedure, not the individual interval. •​An exact interval can be obtained by bracketing the pivot between two quantiles and solving for  	heta . For normal samples this can be used to derive the z -, t - and chi-squared intervals. •​Tests and confidence sets are dual: the values 	heta_{0} not rejected by a family of level- \alpha tests form a 1-\alpha confidence set, and conversely. •​The Wald interval \hat{	heta}_{n}\pm z_{1-\alpha/2}\,\mathop{\mathrm{se}}
olimits(\hat{	heta}% _{n}) asymptotically covers 1-\alpha for all regular models, but the finite-sample coverage may be lower. •​One-sided intervals use z_{1-\alpha} instead of z_{1-\alpha/2} . For two-sided intervals the equal-tailed choice is the norm. Exercise 25.1. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. exponential with rate  	heta>0 and let T=\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_{i} . In exercise 13.1 we have seen that 2	heta T\sim\chi^{2}_{2n} . 1.​Explain why Q=2	heta T is a pivot for  	heta and derive the equal-tailed confidence interval for  	heta with level 1-\alpha . 2.​For n=10 observations \sum_{i}x_{i}=25.0 , compute the 95\% interval, using the values \chi^{2}_{20,0.025}=9.591 and \chi^{2}_{20,0.975}=34.170 . 3.​The MLE for the rate is \hat{	heta}=1/ ar{X} and the standard error is \hat{	heta}/\sqrt{n} , as shown in example 17.4. Compute the Wald interval from proposition 25.7 for the given data and compare the two intervals. Exercise 25.2. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. uniformly distributed on the set [0,	heta] and let M=\max_{i}X_{i} . 1.​Using the density of  M from example 8.10, show that \mathbb{P}_{	heta}(M/	heta\leq u)=u^{n} for 0\leq u\leq 1 and conclude that M/	heta is a pivot. 2.​Show that [M,\ M/\alpha^{1/n}] is a confidence interval for  	heta with level 1-\alpha and compute the interval for n=5 , \max_{i}x_{i}=3.2 and \alpha=0.05 . 3.​Among all intervals [M/b,\ M/a] with 0<a<b\leq 1 and \mathbb{P}_{	heta}(a\leq M/	heta\leq b)=1-\alpha , show that the interval from the previous part is the shortest. (Hint: think about how the length of the interval changes when b is moved and a is adjusted to maintain the same coverage.) Exercise 25.3. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. Poisson with mean  	heta>0 . Then the MLE is \hat{	heta}_{n}= ar{X} and the Fisher information is \mathcal{I}(	heta)=1/	heta from example 11.6. 1.​Find the Wald interval for  	heta with level 1-\alpha and determine the interval for level 95\% when n=20 is the number of counts with \sum_{i}x_{i}=30 . 2.