Skip to content. toolbar Lecture 26 Bayesian Inference: Priors and Posteriors So far the parameter  	heta has been a fixed but unknown number, and a sentence such as “ 	heta is probably close to  0.3 ” had no meaning. In this lecture and in lecture 28 we will see that the parameter itself can be given a probability distribution: the prior, chosen before the data are seen, and the posterior, obtained after the data are seen. (We will see an update in pictures in lecture 27.) 26.1 Prior, Likelihood and Posterior Since 	heta is now a random variable, the model density is a conditional density given the parameter, and in the Bayesian lectures we write it as f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu	heta) , where x=(x_{1},\dots,x_{n}) is the whole data set, so that f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu	heta)=\prod_{i}f(x_{i}\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu	heta) is the likelihood L(	heta) of lecture 4. Definition 26.1. Let 	heta be a random variable with values in  \Theta and density  \pi(	heta) , and assume that given  	heta the data X=(X_{1},\dots,X_{n}) have joint density f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu	heta) . Then the density  \pi is called the prior density (or prior) of  	heta and the conditional density \pi(	heta\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux) of  	heta given X=x is called the posterior density (or posterior). The parameters in the prior are called hyperparameters. From now on, unless otherwise specified, \pi will denote a prior or posterior density. In lecture 19, we used the same letter to denote the mixture weight for a two-component model. The posterior density can be found using Bayes’ theorem as given in proposition A.20 in appendix A, where the parameter plays the role of  A and the data play the role of  B . Lemma 26.2. In the situation of definition 26.1, the posterior density is given by equation (26.1) (26.1) \pi(	heta\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux)= rac{f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu	heta% )\,\pi(	heta)}{m(x)},\qquad	ext{where }m(x)=\int_{\Theta}f(x\mskip 1.0mu|% \mskip 1.0mu\vartheta)\,\pi(\vartheta)\,\mathrm{d}\vartheta is the marginal density of the data. The formula holds for all  x with m(x)>0 . Proof. The joint density of (	heta,X) is given by f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu	heta)\,\pi(	heta) . Integrating out  	heta gives the marginal density m(x) of  X . Dividing the joint density by m(x) gives the conditional density of  	heta given X=x , as claimed. ∎ The denominator m(x) makes the posterior integrate to one and does not involve  	heta ; in practice we never compute it directly, but write equation (26.2) (26.2) \pi(	heta\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux)\propto f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu% 	heta)\,\pi(	heta), where \propto means “equal up to a factor free of  	heta ”, and we try to recognise the right-hand side as the kernel of a known density, whose normalising constant then comes from table A.2. In words, the posterior is proportional to the likelihood times the prior. The posterior is the complete Bayesian answer: a statement like “ \mathbb{P}(	heta>0.3\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux)=0.95 ” now has a meaning. Point estimates are summaries of the posterior (section 26.3), and intervals and tests will be considered in lecture 28. 26.2 Conjugate Priors The rule (26.2) works best if the posterior distribution belongs to the same family of distributions as the prior distribution, so that the update amounts to no more than a change of the hyperparameters. Definition 26.3. A family \mathcal{P} of prior distributions is conjugate for a model  igl{(}f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu	heta)\!\mathrel{ ig{|}}\!	heta\in% \Theta igr{)} , if for every prior in  \mathcal{P} and every data set  x the posterior \pi(	heta\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux) also belongs to  \mathcal{P} . For exponential families, conjugate priors can be written down by inspection: the likelihood depends on the natural parameter  \eta only through the exponent \eta T_{n}-nK(\eta) (lemma 7.5), and a prior with an exponent of the same form reproduces itself. Proposition 26.4. Let f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu\eta)=h(x)\exp igl{(}\eta T(x)-K(\eta) igr{)} with \eta\in\mathcal{N} be a one-parameter exponential family in its natural parametrisation, and for real numbers a and  b let \pi_{a,b}(\eta)\propto\exp igl{(}a\eta-bK(\eta) igr{)},\qquad\eta\in\mathcal% {N}, whenever the right-hand side has a finite integral over  \mathcal{N} . Then these priors are conjugate for the model: if the prior is \pi_{a,b} , the data x_{1},\dots,x_{n} have natural statistic T_{n}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}T(x_{i}) and the marginal density satisfies 0<m(x)<\infty , then the posterior is \pi_{a+T_{n},\,b+n} . Proof. Using (26.2) and lemma 7.5 we find equation equation since \prod_{i}h(x_{i}) does not depend on  \eta . The right-hand side is the kernel of \pi_{a+T_{n},b+n} and by assumption m(x)<\infty this kernel has finite integral and thus is a density. This shows that the posterior is \pi_{a+T_{n},b+n} . This completes the proof. ∎ The update adds the natural statistic to  a and the sample size to  b : the prior acts like b earlier observations with statistic total  a . We now work the recipe for our simplest model in the original parameter. Example 26.5. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. Bernoulli with success probability 	heta\in(0,1) and let the prior be the 	ext{Beta}(a,b) distribution with a,b>0 . From table A.2 we know that this distribution has density \pi(	heta)= rac{	heta^{a-1}(1-	heta)^{b-1}}{B(a,b)},\qquad 0<	heta<1, and the mean of this distribution is a/(a+b) . If T=\sum_{i}x_{i} is the number of successes in the sample, then the likelihood is f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu	heta)=	heta^{T}(1-	heta)^{n-T} , as in example 4.2, and thus we have \pi(	heta\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux)\propto	heta^{T}(1-	heta)^{n-T}\cdot% 	heta^{a-1}(1-	heta)^{b-1}=	heta^{a+T-1}(1-	heta)^{b+n-T-1}, where we have omitted the constant 1/B(a,b) for simplicity. The right-hand side is the kernel of a 	ext{Beta}(a+T,\,b+n-T) density and thus we have found the posterior distribution: the Beta distributions are conjugate for the Bernoulli model, and the update adds the successes to  a and the failures to  b . The posterior mean is given by equation (26.3) (26.3) \mathbb{E}(	heta\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux)= rac{a+T}{a+b+n}= rac{a+b}{a+b+% n}\cdot rac{a}{a+b}+ rac{n}{a+b+n}\cdot ar{x}, which is a weighted average of the prior mean a/(a+b) and the sample proportion  ar{x}=T/n , i.e. the maximum likelihood estimate from example 5.3. The prior can be interpreted as having observed a+b additional samples, of which a were successes. This equivalent sample size is a measure for how the hyperparameters should be chosen in practice (exercise 26.1). As n	o\infty , the weight of the prior decreases and with enough data the prior will be completely forgotten. For a=b=2 , n=10 and T=7 , for example, the posterior distribution is 	ext{Beta}(9,5) with mean 9/14\approx 0.643 , between the prior mean  0.5 and the sample proportion  0.7 . In terms of the log-odds  \eta , the example is an instance of proposition 26.4. Using the same arguments, we can show that normal priors are conjugate for the normal mean with known variance. The cumulant function in this case is a quadratic function of  \eta (exercise 7.4). The result is given here without proof, and the proof, based on completing the square, is given as exercise 26.3. Proposition 26.6. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d.  N(	heta,\sigma^{2}) with known variance  \sigma^{2} and let the prior be 	heta\sim N(m,	au^{2}) . Then the posterior distribution is N(m_{n},	au_{n}^{2}) where  rac{1}{	au_{n}^{2}}= rac{1}{	au^{2}}+ rac{n}{\sigma^{2}}\qquad	ext{and}% \qquad m_{n}= rac{1/	au^{2}}{1/	au^{2}+n/\sigma^{2}}\,m+ rac{n/\sigma^{2}}% {1/	au^{2}+n/\sigma^{2}}\, ar{x}. The reciprocal of a variance is called a precision, and in these terms the proposition is easy to remember: precisions add, and the posterior mean is the precision-weighted average of the prior mean and   ar{x} . Again, the prior washes out as n	o\infty . 26.3 Point Estimates from the Posterior If a single number is required for  	heta , the posterior mean, median and mode can all be used. If the errors are measured in terms of squared error, as in lecture 2, the posterior mean is the optimal choice. Lemma 26.7. Assume that the posterior distribution has finite variance. Then the posterior mean \hat{	heta}_{B}=\mathbb{E}(	heta\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux) minimises the posterior expected squared error, i.e. for every number  d we have \mathbb{E} igl{(}(	heta-d)^{2}\!\mathrel{ ig{|}}\!x igr{)}\geq\mathbb{E}%  igl{(}(	heta-\hat{	heta}_{B})^{2}\!\mathrel{ ig{|}}\!x igr{)}=\mathop{% \mathrm{Var}}
olimits(	heta\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux), and the equality holds only for d=\hat{	heta}_{B} . Proof. Adding and subtracting \hat{	heta}_{B} inside the square, as in the proof of lemma 2.7, we get \mathbb{E} igl{(}(	heta-d)^{2}\!\mathrel{ ig{|}}\!x igr{)}=\mathbb{E} igl% {(}(	heta-\hat{	heta}_{B})^{2}\!\mathrel{ ig{|}}\!x igr{)}+2(\hat{	heta}_% {B}-d)\,\mathbb{E}(	heta-\hat{	heta}_{B}\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux)+(\hat{% 	heta}_{B}-d)^{2}, where all expectations are taken with respect to the posterior. The middle term vanishes, because \mathbb{E}(	heta\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux)=\hat{	heta}_{B} , and the last term is non-negative and zero only for d=\hat{	heta}_{B} . This completes the proof. ∎ The estimator \hat{	heta}_{B} is called the Bayes estimator (under squared error) for  	heta . The lemma is a statement about fixed data. For fixed  	heta , and varying data, exercise 26.4 shows that the Bernoulli \hat{	heta}_{B} is biased but consistent, and that neither the Bernoulli estimator nor  ar{X} has smaller mean squared error for all  	heta . 26.4 Flat, Improper and Jeffreys Priors Without prior knowledge to encode, the obvious choice is the flat prior which is a constant density on  \Theta . This prior does not prefer any value of  	heta . For the Bernoulli model this prior is 	ext{Beta}(1,1) and from example 26.5 we know that the posterior mean is (T+1)/(n+2) , which is close to   ar{x} but is shifted towards  1/2 . Since a constant density on an unbounded parameter space has infinite integral, the flat prior is improper. Nevertheless, this prior is still in use today: if f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu	heta)\,\pi(	heta) has finite integral, it is normalised and used as the posterior. The finiteness of the integral must be checked in each case (exercise 26.2). For the normal mean, the flat prior on  \mathbb{R} is the limit 	au	o\infty in proposition 26.6 and in this case leads to the proper posterior N( ar{x},\sigma^{2}/n) . The flat prior has a more basic defect: flatness depends on the parametrisation. If  	heta is uniform on (0,1) , then \psi=	heta^{2} has density 1/(2\sqrt{\psi}) by proposition A.19, and a prior which claims to know nothing about  	heta claims to know something about  	heta^{2} . The Fisher information provides a parametrisation-free rule, since proposition 11.10 shows how it transforms. Definition 26.8. In the situation of section 11.1, the Jeffreys prior for  	heta is given by \pi_{J}(	heta)\propto\sqrt{\mathcal{I}(	heta)},\qquad	heta\in\Theta, where \mathcal{I}(	heta) is the Fisher information of an observation. This prior is justified by the following invariance property, which the flat prior lacks. Proposition 26.9. Let \psi=g(	heta) where g\colon\Theta	o g(\Theta) is a continuously differentiable bijection with g^{\prime}(	heta)
