alue is between the maximum likelihood estimate  T/n and the estimate (T+1)/(n+2) obtained using the flat prior. For large  n , all three values coincide. For location and scale models the Jeffreys prior is the same for every base density  f_{0} . Proposition 26.11. Let f_{0} be a fixed density such that the following models satisfy the conditions of section 11.1. 1.​Consider the location model f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu	heta)=f_{0}(x-	heta) with 	heta\in\mathbb{R} . The Fisher information in this model is constant as a function of  	heta and thus the Jeffreys prior is flat, \pi_{J}(	heta)\propto 1 on  \mathbb{R} . 2.​Consider the scale model f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu\sigma)=f_{0}(x/\sigma)/\sigma with \sigma>0 . The Fisher information in this model is \mathcal{I}(\sigma)=c/\sigma^{2} for a constant  c>0 . Thus, the Jeffreys prior is \pi_{J}(\sigma)\propto 1/\sigma on (0,\infty) . Both priors are improper. Exercise 26.5 verifies both statements by writing the score as a function of the standardised observation. The normal mean with known variance is a location model, so its Jeffreys prior is flat. Similarly, the normal standard deviation with known mean is a scale model with \mathcal{I}(\sigma)=2/\sigma^{2} . Summary. •​The data can be used to update the prior density  \pi(	heta) to the posterior \pi(	heta\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux)\propto f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu% 	heta)\,\pi(	heta) . The normalising constant can be found by recognising the kernel of a known density. •​Priors are conjugate for a family if the posterior always belongs to the same family. For a one-parameter exponential family in its natural parametrisation, the priors \exp(a\eta-bK(\eta)) are conjugate. Beta priors are conjugate for the Bernoulli model, and normal priors are conjugate for the normal mean with known variance. •​The posterior mean is a weighted average of the prior mean and the maximum likelihood estimate. As n increases, the prior weight decreases. The posterior mean is the Bayes estimator for 	heta under squared error. •​A flat prior is not invariant under reparametrisation, but the Jeffreys prior \pi_{J}(	heta)\propto\sqrt{\mathcal{I}(	heta)} is. The Jeffreys prior is 	ext{Beta}(1/2,1/2) for the Bernoulli model, is flat for a location parameter, and is proportional to 1/\sigma for a scale parameter. An improper prior can only be used if the posterior is proper. Exercise 26.1. A Bernoulli model is to be fitted with a 	ext{Beta}(a,b) prior for the success probability  	heta . 1.​Find a and  b such that the prior mean is 0.3 and the equivalent sample size a+b is  10 . You can find the prior standard deviation in table A.2. 2.​Find a and  b such that the prior has mean 0.3 and standard deviation  0.1 . What is the equivalent sample size for this prior? 3.​For the prior in the first part of the question, a sample of size n=20 has T=9 successes. Determine the posterior distribution and the posterior mean. Write the posterior mean as a weighted average of the prior mean and   ar{x} . Exercise 26.2. The Haldane prior for the Bernoulli success probability is the limit a,b	o 0 of the 	ext{Beta}(a,b) prior, i.e. the function \pi(	heta)\propto	heta^{-1}(1-	heta)^{-1} for 0<	heta<1 . 1.​Show that this prior is improper. 2.​Show that the formal posterior \pi(	heta\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux)\propto	heta^{T-1}(1-	heta)^{n-T-1} is a proper distribution if and only if 0<T<n . Identify the resulting distribution. 3.​For 0<T<n , determine the posterior mean and compare it to the maximum likelihood estimate. 4.​Describe what happens for T=0 . Exercise 26.3. Using the following steps, prove proposition 26.6. 1.​Show that the likelihood, as a function of  	heta , satisfies f(x\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mu	heta)\propto\exp igl{(}-n(	heta- ar{x})^{2}/(% 2\sigma^{2}) igr{)} . 2.​Multiply the prior density with this function and show, by completing the square in  	heta , that \pi(	heta\mskip 1.0mu|\mskip 1.0mux)\propto\exp\Bigl{(}- rac{(	heta-m_{n})^% {2}}{2	au_{n