Skip to content. toolbar Lecture 29 Standard Tests Revisited The most commonly used statistical tests, the z -test, the t -test, the chi-squared test for a variance and the F -test, are often taught as recipes. In this lecture we will consider these tests again, from the perspective of the module: each of these tests is based on a pivot in the sense of lecture 25, and the test is performed by comparing a statistic to a quantile from a table. We will work through the one-sample t -test in detail, including how to read the tables, and will state the variance, paired, two-sample and F -tests in a similar way. We will conclude by giving a table which shows which test is appropriate for which question. 29.1 Tests from Pivots Every test in this lecture is based on the following principle: the test statistic is known to follow a certain distribution under the null hypothesis, and the test rejects if the statistic exceeds a quantile of this distribution, taken from a table. Using the language of lecture 25, the test statistic is a pivot Q(X,	heta) for the hypothesised value 	heta=	heta_{0} and the test is the one which, when inverted by lemma 25.6, gives the confidence interval based on this pivot. We use the notation z_{p} , t_{k,p} and \chi^{2}_{k,p} for the quantiles from lecture 25, so that \mathbb{P}(T\leq t_{k,p})=p for T\sim t_{k} . A table shows these quantiles for a few values of  p . For a one-sided test at level  \alpha , we need the (1-\alpha) -quantile, and for a two-sided test at the same level we need the (1-\alpha/2) -quantile. A p -value in the sense of definition 20.8 cannot be found exactly in a table, but can be bracketed between two tabulated levels; we will see an example of this approach in example 29.2, below. The most basic example is the z -test from lecture 20: if X_{1},\dots,X_{n}\sim N(\mu,\sigma^{2}) are i.i.d. with known  \sigma^{2} , then Z=\sqrt{n}\,( ar{X}-\mu_{0})/\sigma is standard normally distributed under H_{0}\colon\mu=\mu_{0} and in examples 20.6 and 20.7 we can reject for Z>z_{1-\alpha} or |Z|>z_{1-\alpha/2} for the one-sided and two-sided alternative, respectively. The simplicity of this test is countered by the fact that the variance of the data is known. The rest of the lecture is concerned with how to replace  Z when \sigma^{2} is unknown. 29.2 The One-Sample t -Test Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d.  N(\mu,\sigma^{2}) , where both parameters are unknown, and let  ar{X} and S^{2} be the sample mean and sample variance, respectively. If we replace \sigma in the definition of Z by the estimate  S , we get the test statistic equation (29.1) (29.1) T= rac{\sqrt{n}\,( ar{X}-\mu_{0})}{S}. In example 25.4 we have seen that for \mu=\mu_{0} this is the ratio of the standard normal variable \sqrt{n}\,( ar{X}-\mu_{0})/\sigma to the square root of V/(n-1) , where V=(n-1)S^{2}/\sigma^{2}\sim\chi^{2}_{n-1} by proposition A.4, and that the two values are independent of each other by corollary 10.10. Consequently, T\sim t_{n-1} by definition A.5. This leads to the following tests. Proposition 29.1. Let n\geq 2 and let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d.  N(\mu,\sigma^{2}) , where the parameters \mu and \sigma^{2} are unknown, \mu_{0}\in\mathbb{R} and let T be given by (29.1). Then the three tests of H_{0}\colon\mu=\mu_{0} , which reject if T>t_{n-1,1-\alpha},\qquad T<-t_{n-1,1-\alpha},\qquad	ext{or}\qquad|T|>t_{n-1,% 1-\alpha/2}, for the alternatives \mu>\mu_{0} , \mu<\mu_{0} and \mu
eq\mu_{0} , respectively, have size  \alpha , independent of the value of  \sigma^{2} . Proof. Under  H_{0} we have T\sim t_{n-1} for every value of  \sigma^{2} , as shown above. By the definition of the quantile we have \mathbb{P}(T>t_{n-1,1-\alpha})=1-(1-\alpha)=\alpha . This is the size of the first test. The density of the t distribution is symmetric about zero and thus we have \mathbb{P}(T<-t_{n-1,1-\alpha})=\mathbb{P}(T>t_{n-1,1-\alpha})=\alpha for the second test and for the third test we have \mathbb{P}(|T|>t_{n-1,1-\alpha/2})=2\cdot\alpha/2=\alpha . Since these probabilities do not depend on  \sigma^{2} , the proof is complete. ∎ These are the one-sided and two-sided one-sample t -tests. Since t_{n-1,p}>z_{p} for every p>1/2 , the t -test requires a larger standardised deviation than the z -test before it can reject the hypothesis, and this is the price we pay for estimating  \sigma . As n increases, this price decreases as the t_{n-1} distribution gets closer to the standard normal distribution. As in lecture 20, the one-sided tests have level  \alpha for the composite null hypotheses \mu\leq\mu_{0} and \mu\geq\mu_{0} , since the probability of rejection increases as \mu moves into the alternative. Example 29.2. A quantity has nominal value  10 , and ten measurements give 10.2,\ 9.7,\ 11.1,\ 10.8,\ 10.4,\ 9.9,\ 11.3,\ 10.6,\ 10.1,\ 10.9. Assume that the measurements are i.i.d.  N(\mu,\sigma^{2}) . We want to test H_{0}\colon\mu=10 against H_{1}\colon\mu
eq 10 at level \alpha=0.05 . The sum of the observations is 105 , so that  ar{x}=10.5 ; the sum of the squared deviations from   ar{x} is 2.52 , so that s^{2}=2.52/9=0.28 and s=0.529 . The observed value of the test statistic is t= rac{\sqrt{10}\,(10.5-10)}{0.529}=2.99. The row corresponding to n-1=9 degrees of freedom in the t -table shows that we have t_{9,0.95}=1.833 , t_{9,0.975}=2.262 , t_{9,0.99}=2.821 and t_{9,0.995}=3.250 . For a two-sided test with level  0.05 we need t_{9,0.975}=2.262 and since |t|=2.99>2.262 we can reject  H_{0} : the data are not compatible with a mean of  10 . The same row also brackets the p -value. The observed value 2.99 falls between t_{9,0.99} and t_{9,0.995} and thus the two-sided p -value, 2\,\mathbb{P}(T\geq 2.99) for T\sim t_{9} , is between 0.01 and 0.02 ; the two-sided test also rejects at level  0.02 but not at level  0.01 . If instead the question had been whether the measurements were high, the one-sided test for H_{0}\colon\mu=10 against H_{1}\colon\mu>10 at level 0.05 would have compared t=2.99 to t_{9,0.95}=1.833 and would have rejected  H_{0} , with a p -value between 0.005 and 0.01 . The t -test is the test obtained by applying the likelihood-ratio principle from lecture 23. In exercise 23.1 the generalised likelihood-ratio statistic for H_{0}\colon\mu=\mu_{0} , where \sigma^{2} is unknown for both hypotheses, is found to be -2\log\Lambda=n\log igl{(}1+T^{2}/(n-1) igr{)} , which is a strictly increasing function of  T^{2} . Thus, rejecting large values of -2\log\Lambda corresponds to the two-sided t -test. Following the general method of lecture 23, we would use the critical value from the \chi^{2}_{1} approximation of Wilks’ theorem, theorem 23.3; however, in this case the null distribution of  T is known exactly and the t_{n-1} quantile is the exact critical value for every  n . As n gets large, the two approaches will be equal, since n\log(1+t^{2}/(n-1))	o t^{2} and t_{n-1,1-\alpha/2}	o z_{1-\alpha/2} and then z_{1