Skip to content. toolbar Lecture 5 Maximum Likelihood Estimation In the previous lecture we have seen that for the exponential model, the parameter value which maximises the likelihood is the one which ‘best’ explains the data. In this lecture we will make this idea into the main estimation method of the module: we define the maximum likelihood estimator, practise computing it for the families from tables 1.1 and 1.2, and will see problems where more thought is needed. We will see that the estimator is not unbiased in general, but is invariant under reparametrisation; the deeper justification of the method will be given in lectures 13, 16 and 17. 5.1 The Maximum Likelihood Estimator Example 4.5 already contains the idea: for the exponential sample the log-likelihood increases up to 	heta=1/ ar{x} and then decreases, so this value fits the data best. We now formalise this principle. Definition 5.1. A maximum likelihood estimator (MLE) for  	heta is an estimator \hat{	heta}=\hat{	heta}(X_{1},\dots,X_{n}) , which maximises the likelihood function over the parameter space, i.e. which satisfies L(\hat{	heta})\geq L(	heta)\qquad	ext{for all $	heta\in\Theta$.} Since the logarithm is strictly increasing, \hat{	heta} maximises  L if and only if it maximises the log-likelihood  \ell and for the reasons given in lecture 4 we mostly work with  \ell . The definition is careful to avoid the problem of a maximum not existing or not being unique. For example, the likelihood could increase as the edge of the parameter space is approached, so that no maximum exists. Similarly, the maximum may not be unique. In such cases, any maximiser can be used. For the standard families of models and typical data, neither of these problems will occur and it will be safe to speak of “the” MLE. If the set \Theta is an interval and the log-likelihood function is differentiable, then calculus can be used to find the MLE: at a maximum inside the interval  \Theta , the derivative of  \ell equals zero and thus the MLE satisfies the likelihood equation \ell^{\prime}(	heta)=0, i.e. the MLE is a zero of the score function from definition 4.4. The general procedure is to find the log-likelihood function, to solve the likelihood equation and to verify that the solution is a maximum, for example by checking that \ell^{\prime\prime}(	heta)<0 or by checking that the score changes from positive to negative. Warning. The solution of the likelihood equation only gives interior critical points. These could be minima or inflection points and the checking step is required to avoid wrong answers. Also, the above procedure assumes that the maximum is interior and that \ell is differentiable. We will see that these assumptions can be violated. 5.2 Worked Examples We start our analysis by considering the “running families” of the module. Example 5.2. In example 4.5, the score of the exponential model equals zero at 	heta=1/ ar{x} . Since \ell^{\prime\prime}(	heta)=-n/	heta^{2}<0 for all 	heta>0 , the function \ell is strictly concave and this root is the only global maximum. Thus, the MLE for the rate of the exponential distribution is \hat{	heta}= rac{1}{ ar{X}}. This coincides with the method of moments estimator from example 4.7. Example 5.3. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be an i.i.d. Bernoulli sample with success probability  	heta , and let s=\sum_{i}x_{i} be the observed number of successes. As in example 4.2 we have the likelihood L(	heta)=	heta^{s}(1-	heta)^{n-s} and thus \ell(	heta)=s\log	heta+(n-s)\log(1-	heta),\qquad	heta\in(0,1). Assume first 0<s<n . Then the likelihood equation is \ell^{\prime}(	heta)= rac{s}{	heta}- rac{n-s}{1-	heta}=0. This equation implies s(1-	heta)=(n-s)	heta and the only solution to this equation is 	heta=s/n . Since \ell^{\prime\prime}(	heta)=-s/	heta^{2}-(n-s)/(1-	heta)^{2}<0 everywhere, this root is the global maximum and the MLE for the proportion of successes is \hat{	heta}= rac{s}{n}= ar{X}. For the dataset from example 4.2, seven heads in ten tosses, we get \hat{	heta}=0.7 , the value which we already recognised in the example. Remark. The assumption 0<s<n is made, because at the two extremes the MLE does not exist: for s=n the function \ell(	heta)=n\log	heta is strictly increasing on (0,1) and the supremum is approached at the boundary point 	heta=1 , which is outside the open parameter space, and thus there is no maximiser. This is a simple case of the existence caveat from definition 5.1. In this case we could, for example, report the boundary value s/n=1 or else extend the parameter space to the closed interval. Example 5.4. Consider X_{1},\dots,X_{n}\sim N(\mu,\sigma^{2}) , where both parameters are unknown. Then we have 	heta=(\mu,\sigma^{2}) . Taking logarithms we get \ell(\mu,\sigma^{2})=- rac{n}{2}\log(2\pi\sigma^{2})- rac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}% \sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_{i}-\mu)^{2}. Since we have a two-dimensional parameter, the likelihood equation translates to a system of two equations: we set both partial derivatives equal to zero. Taking derivatives with respect to  \mu we find  rac{\partial\ell}{\partial\mu}= rac{1}{\sigma^{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_{i}-\mu), which is zero only for \mu= ar{x} . Since the sum of squares \sum_{i}(x_{i}-\mu)^{2} takes its minimum for \mu= ar{x} , this value maximises \ell over  \mu for every fixed  \sigma^{2} and we can substitute \mu= ar{x} to find the maximum of the remaining function of one variable. Let v=\sigma^{2} and W=\sum_{i}(x_{i}- ar{x})^{2} . Then we have \ell( ar{x},v)=- rac{n}{2}\log(2\pi v)- rac{W}{2v}, with derivative  rac{\partial\ell}{\partial v}=- rac{n}{2v}+ rac{W}{2v^{2}}= rac{W-nv}{2v^% {2}}, which is positive for v<W/n and negative for v>W/n . Thus, the maximum is at v=W/n . Hence, the MLE is the pair \hat{\mu}= ar{X}\qquad	ext{and}\qquad\hat{\sigma}^{2}= rac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^% {n}(X_{i}- ar{X})^{2}. This is the plug-in estimator, obtained by using the method of moments in example 4.8, with divisor  n : for the normal distribution, both methods give the same result for both coordinates. In particular, the MLE for the variance is the biased estimator from section 2.3, where we showed that the bias in this case was harmless. Whether the bias is a general property of maximum likelihood is a question we will consider later. 5.3 When the Recipe Fails Both assumptions in the recipe, i.e. an interior maximum and differentiable log-likelihood, break down for the uniform model. In this case we have to return to definition 5.1 and maximise the expression directly. Example 5.5. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n}\sim	ext{Uniform}(0,	heta) be i.i.d., as in example 1.2. From the joint density found in exercise 1.3, the likelihood is L(	heta)= egin{cases}	heta^{-n}&	ext{if $	heta\geq\max_{i}x_{i}$,}\ 0&	ext{otherwise.}\end{cases} Calculus is useless here: on the region where L is positive its derivative -n	heta^{-n-1} is negative everywhere, so the likelihood equation has no solution, and at 	heta=\max_{i}x_{i} the function is not even continuous. But maximising directly is easy: the likelihood is zero for 	heta<\max_{i}x_{i} and strictly decreasing for 	heta\geq\max_{i}x_{i} , and thus largest at the left edge of the positive region. The MLE is the sample maximum, \hat{	heta}=\max_{i}X_{i}. This identifies the estimator we have been tracking since exercise 1.3, which by exercise 4.4 beats the method of moments estimator 2 ar{X} in mean squared error for every n\geq 3 . The likelihood also rules out impossible parameter values automatically: for the dataset x_{1}=0.1 , x_{2}=0.2 , x_{3}=0.9 of lecture 4, any 	heta<0.9 has likelihood zero, so the contradiction the method of moments overlooked cannot arise. The uniform model fails the recipe in an obvious way; a failure which is harder to spot is a likelihood equation with several roots. Consider the t location model with 
