olimits(% \hat{	heta})=(n-1)/(n+2)<1 for all n\geq 3 . Thus, after removing the bias, this estimator is strictly better. The same exercise shows that a third scaling of \hat{	heta} would be even better, similar to how the divisor n+1 in the normal distribution variance was chosen. For the normal variance the biased divisor- n estimator was better than the unbiased sample variance  S^{2} (section 2.3); for the exponential rate the bias-corrected estimator is better. Neither result is a theorem: the bias-variance trade-off can go either way, and the only way to know which is better is to compute both mean squared errors. In favour of the MLE we note that the bias decreases to zero as the sample size increases, and in lecture 17 we will see that for large sample size the MLE is in some sense the best estimator. 5.5 Invariance Under Reparametrisation Often the quantity we want to estimate is not the parameter itself, but a function of the parameter, e.g. a probability, a mean or a median. The following proposition shows that under maximum likelihood such quantities transform together with the parameter. Proposition 5.6 (Invariance of the MLE). Let g\colon\Theta	o\Psi be a bijection and let \hat{	heta} be a maximum likelihood estimator for  	heta . Then g(\hat{	heta}) is a maximum likelihood estimator for the transformed parameter \psi=g(	heta) . The idea of the proof is that re-labelling the parameter does not change the family of distributions: in terms of  \psi the likelihood is 	ilde{L}(\psi)=L igl{(}g^{-1}(\psi) igr{)} , so maximising 	ilde{L} over  \Psi is the same maximisation as before. Writing this down is exercise 5.5. Remark. If g is not a bijection, then the likelihood of  \psi is defined to be the maximal likelihood of the values of  	heta which map to this value. While the statement \hat{\psi}=g(\hat{	heta}) is still true, we will not require the general case in this module. As an application, exercise 5.8 finds the MLE for the median waiting time m=(\log 2)/	heta of the exponential model: since 	heta\mapsto(\log 2)/	heta is a bijection, proposition 5.6 gives \hat{m}=(\log 2)\, ar{X} with no further maximisation. The exercise also contains a surprise: \hat{m} is unbiased, despite the MLE for the rate being biased. In general, unbiasedness is lost under nonlinear reparametrisation, and thus there is no analogue of proposition 5.6 for unbiasedness. Since we have seen that the MLE can be biased, non-unique and even non-existent, we need to do more work to understand the method. The arguments for the validity of the MLE can be found in lectures 13, 16 and 17: the main result is the Cramer–Rao bound, which no unbiased estimator can violate. For large sample sizes, the MLE is shown to be consistent and to asymptotically achieve this bound. In preparation for these arguments, lecture 8 summarises the observation from example 4.3 that the likelihood function can be collapsed to a small number of data summaries. Summary. •​The maximum likelihood estimator \hat{	heta} is the parameter value which maximises the likelihood (or log-likelihood) of the data. •​For smooth problems, where the maximum is an interior point of the parameter space, the MLE can be found by solving the likelihood equation \ell^{\prime}(	heta)=0 and verifying that the root is a maximum. For the standard families of models this gives  ar{X} (Bernoulli and Poisson), 1/ ar{X} (exponential rate) and  igl{(} ar{X},(1/n)\sum_{i}(X_{i}- ar{X})^{2} igr{)} (normal). •​If the recipe fails, the definition can still be used: for the uniform distribution the maximum sits at the boundary, at \hat{	heta}=\max_{i}X_{i} , and for the t location model with two observations far apart the MLE is not unique. •​The MLE is not unbiased in general. Whether removing the bias improves the mean squared error depends on the model: for the exponential rate the corrected estimator wins, reversing the normal-variance comparison of lecture 2. •​The MLE is invariant under reparametrisation: the MLE for g(	heta) is g(\hat{	heta}) . Unbiasedness is not invariant. •​The full justification of the method, including efficiency and large-sample optimality, follows in lectures 13, 16 and 17. Exercise 5.1. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be an i.i.d. sample from the geometric distribution with parameter p\in(0,1) , i.e.  f(x;p)=(1-p)^{x-1}p for x\in\mathbb{N} as in exercise 4.2. 1.​Show that the log-likelihood function is given by \ell(p)=n\log p+ igl{(}\sum_{i}x_{i}-n igr{)}\log(1-p) . 2.​Solve the likelihood equation and show that your solution is a maximum. Deduce that the MLE is \hat{p}=1/ ar{X} . 3.​Discuss your results in the light of the method of moments estimator from exercise 4.2. Exercise 5.2. Let X_{1},\dots,X_{n} be i.i.d. with density f(x;	heta)=	heta^{-2}x\,e^{-x/	heta} for x\geq 0 , where 	heta>0 . This is the gamma distribution with known shape  2 and scale  	heta , i.e.  	ext{Gamma}(2,1/	heta) in the rate parametrisation from table A.2. 1.​Find the MLE \hat{	heta} for  	heta . 2.​Show that your estimator \hat{	heta} is unbiased. 3.​Determine \mathop{\mathrm{MSE}}
