Skip to content. toolbar Lecture 7 Exponential Families In lectures 4 and 5 we repeatedly collapsed products of n densities to expressions which summarised the data using only one or two sums. This lecture explains the reason for this coincidence: almost all families of models considered here have the same algebraic shape, and models of this shape are called exponential families. This shape allows us to define the data summary used in the likelihood, the moments of this summary, and the smoothness required for the later theory, and many of the results in the following weeks are almost automatic for this class. 7.1 Recognising the Shape We start by considering a familiar distribution and then rewrite its probability weights so that the data and the parameter are as far apart as possible. For the Bernoulli distribution with x\in\{0,1\} we can write equation equation Here we have written the weights as the exponential of the product of one function of  	heta and one function of  x , minus a function of only  	heta . The Poisson weights and the exponential density can be written in a similar way: we have 	heta^{x}=e^{x\log	heta} and 	heta e^{-	heta x}=e^{-	heta x+\log	heta} , up to a factor depending on  x . We will give a name to this structure. Definition 7.1. A parametric model  igl{(}f(x;	heta)\!\mathrel{ ig{|}}\!	heta\in\Theta igr{)} with \Theta\subseteq\mathbb{R} is a one-parameter exponential family, if the density or the probability weights can be written as f(x;	heta)=h(x)\exp igl{(}\eta(	heta)\,T(x)-B(	heta) igr{)} for all  x and all 	heta\in\Theta , where h\geq 0 and T are functions of  x alone and \eta and B are functions of  	heta alone. In particular, the support \{x\mid f(x;	heta)>0\} is \{x\mid h(x)>0\} and does not depend on  	heta . The statistic  T(X) is called the natural statistic and \eta(	heta) is called the natural parameter of the family. Since the exponential is always positive, f(x;	heta) is positive if and only if h(x) is. This proves the statement about the support. The representation is not unique, since we can replace \eta by c\eta and T by T/c for any constant  c
