nteresting: the natural parameter values of the normal distribution fill the open half-plane \{(\eta_{1},\eta_{2})\mid\eta_{2}<0\} (example 7.9) and the natural parameter values of the gamma distribution fill an open quadrant (exercise 7.3), but the natural parameter values of N(	heta,	heta^{2}) with 	heta>0 , where the standard deviation equals the mean, form a curve in  \mathbb{R}^{2} which does not contain an open set and thus the two-parameter family in this case is not of full rank (exercise 7.7). 7.4 What the Structure Buys The definitions in this lecture have applications in many areas. In lecture 8 we see that the natural statistic  T_{n} of a sample is sufficient and lemma 7.5 is essentially the whole proof. In lecture 10 we see that full rank implies that T_{n} is complete and we use the Lehmann–Scheffe theorem to find the best unbiased estimators. In lecture 11 we find that the Fisher information for the natural parameter equals K^{\prime\prime}(\eta) . In lecture 13 we see that the estimators which achieve the Cramer–Rao bound are, up to affine transformations, natural statistics. Finally, in lecture 26 we find that conjugate priors arise because the likelihood (7.2) depends on the data only via T_{n} and  n . Exponential families also satisfy the regularity conditions from lectures 11, 16 and 17: the support of the distribution is fixed, the density is smooth in  	heta whenever \eta and  B are, and we have differentiation under the integral sign as in the proof of lemma 7.7. The log-likelihood \ell(\eta)=\sum_{i}\log h(x_{i})+\eta T_{n}-nK(\eta) has second derivative -nK^{\prime\prime}(\eta)=-n\mathop{\mathrm{Var}}
olimits_{\eta} igl{(}T(X)%  igr{)}<0 , since T(X) is not constant, and thus is strictly concave in  \eta . For this reason, the likelihood equation from exercise 7.5 has at most one root and this root is then the maximum likelihood estimator. This shows that the uniqueness hypothesis of the consistency theorem from lecture 16 holds for the whole class. Summary. •​A one-parameter exponential family has density of the form f(x;	heta)=h(x)\exp igl{(}\eta(	heta)T(x)-B(	heta) igr{)} , where the natural statistic is  T , the natural parameter is  \eta and the support of the distribution does not depend on  	heta . Examples of exponential families include the Bernoulli, Poisson, exponential, normal and gamma distributions. The uniform distribution is not an exponential family. •​An i.i.d. sample from an exponential family is again an exponential family. In this case, the natural statistic is T_{n}=\sum_{i}T(X_{i}) and the likelihood depends on the data only through  T_{n} . •​In the natural parametrisation f(x;\eta)=h(x)\exp(\eta T(x)-K(\eta)) the cumulant function K can be used to find the moments of the natural statistic: we have \mathbb{E}_{\eta}(T(X))=K^{\prime}(\eta) and \mathop{\mathrm{Var}}
olimits_{\eta}(T(X))=K^{\prime\prime}(\eta) . •​A family is of full rank, if the values of the natural parameter corresponding to an open set of the correct dimension can be achieved. This condition is required for completeness in lecture 10. •​By construction, exponential families satisfy the regularity conditions of the later theory and the log-likelihood function is strictly concave in the natural parameter. Exercise 7.1. Let X be geometrically distributed with parameter p\in(0,1) , i.e.  f(x;p)=(1-p)^{x-1}p for x\in\mathbb{N} , as in exercises 4.2 and 5.1. 1.​Show that this distribution is a one-parameter exponential family and identify the functions h , T , \eta and  B . What is the natural statistic of an i.i.d. sample X_{1},\dots,X_{n} ? 2.​Determine the natural parameter space  \mathcal{N} and the cumulant function  K . Check that B(p)=K igl{(}\eta(p) igr{)} . Is this family of full rank? 3.​Using lemma 7.7, determine the mean and variance of  X and compare the result with table A.1. Exercise 7.2. Let X be binomially distributed with a known number  m of trials and unknown success probability 	heta\in(0,1) , i.e.  f(x;	heta)= inom{m}{x}	heta^{x}(1-	heta)^{m-x} for x\in\{0,1,\dots,m\} . 1.​Show that this distribution is a one-parameter exponential family, with the same natural parameter as the Bernoulli distribution from example 7.2, and identify the functions h , T and  B . 2.​Determine the natural parameter space and the cumulant function. 3.​Using lemma 7.7, determine the mean and variance of  X , and the mean and variance of the natural statistic T_{n} for an i.i.d. sample of size  n . Exercise 7.3. Let X be gamma distributed, X\sim	ext{Gamma}(\alpha, eta) with shape \alpha>0 and rate   eta>0 , both unknown, i.e. with density given by f(x;\alpha, eta)= eta^{\alpha}x^{\alpha-1}e^{- eta x}/\Gamma(\alpha) for x>0 , as given in table A.2. 1.​Show that this model is a two-parameter exponential family, as given in definition 7.8, with h(x)=1 for x>0 and h(x)=0 